"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"388.1554"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00341"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"388.1554"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00342"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"471.3137"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00342"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"471.3137"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00343"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"414.1613"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Diltiazem is metabolized by and acts as an inhibitor of the CYP3A4 enzyme."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00343"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"414.1613"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Diltiazem is metabolized by and acts as an inhibitor of the CYP3A4 enzyme."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00344"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"263.1674"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00344"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"263.1674"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00186"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"320.0119"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00187"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"295.1784"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Rapidly metabolized by hydrolysis of the ester linkage, chiefly by the esterases in the cytosol of red blood cells and not by plasma cholinesterases or red cell membrane acetylcholinesterase. Mainly in red blood cells to a free acid metabolite (with 1/1500 the activity of esmolol) and methanol."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00187"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"295.1784"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Rapidly metabolized by hydrolysis of the ester linkage, chiefly by the esterases in the cytosol of red blood cells and not by plasma cholinesterases or red cell membrane acetylcholinesterase. Mainly in red blood cells to a free acid metabolite (with 1/1500 the activity of esmolol) and methanol."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00188"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"384.1969"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"In vitro studies with human liver microsomes and human cDNA-expressed cytochrome P450 isozymes indicate that bortezomib is primarily oxidatively metabolized via cytochrome P450 enzymes 3A4, 2C19, and 1A2, while bortezomib metabolism by CYP 2D6 and 2C9 enzymes is minor. The major metabolic pathway is deboronation to form 2 deboronated metabolites that subsequently undergo hydroxylation to several metabolites which are inactive as 26S proteasome inhibitors."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00189"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"144.0342"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"About 90% of a dose is metabolized in the liver. Some ethchlorvynol may also be metabolized in the kidneys. Ethchlorvynol and metabolites undergo extensive enterohepatic recirculation."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00189"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"144.0342"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"About 90% of a dose is metabolized in the liver. Some ethchlorvynol may also be metabolized in the kidneys. Ethchlorvynol and metabolites undergo extensive enterohepatic recirculation."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00191"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"149.1204"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00191"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"149.1204"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00193"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"263.1885"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"The major metabolic pathways appear to be N- and O- demethylation and glucuronidation or sulfation in the liver. One metabolite (O-desmethyltramadol, denoted M1) is pharmacologically active in animal models."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00115"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"1359.6065"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00116"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"445.171"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00117"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"155.0695"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00117"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"155.0695"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00118"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"398.1372"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Significant first-pass metabolism in the liver. Approximately 50% of S-Adenosylmethionine (SAMe) is metabolized in the liver. SAMe is metabolized to S-adenosylhomocysteine, which is then metabolized to homocysteine. Homocysteine can either be metabolized to cystathionine and then cysteine or to methionine. The cofactor in the metabolism of homocysteine to cysteine is vitamin B6. Cofactors for the metabolism of homocysteine to methionine are folic acid, vitamin B12 and betaine."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00119"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"88.016"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"In the liver, pyruvate is metabolized via several pathways."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00120"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"165.079"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic. L-phenylalanine that is not metabolized in the liver is distributed via the systemic circulation to the various tissues of the body, where it undergoes metabolic reactions similar to those that take place in the liver."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00120"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"165.079"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic. L-phenylalanine that is not metabolized in the liver is distributed via the systemic circulation to the various tissues of the body, where it undergoes metabolic reactions similar to those that take place in the liver."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00121"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"244.0882"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00121"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"244.0882"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00122"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"104.1075"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00123"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"146.1055"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00123"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"146.1055"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00209"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"427.1914"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Not fully defined"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00210"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"412.2038"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Metabolized mainly by O-demethylation, hydroxylation and conjugation, and excretion is primarily by the biliary route."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00212"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"630.3451"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00213"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"383.0751"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Pantoprazole is extensively metabolized in the liver through the cytochrome P450 (CYP) system. The main metabolic pathway is demethylation, by CYP2C19, with subsequent sulfation; other metabolic pathways include oxidation by CYP3A4. There is no evidence that any of the pantoprazole metabolites have significant pharmacologic activity."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00213"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"383.0751"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Pantoprazole is extensively metabolized in the liver through the cytochrome P450 (CYP) system. The main metabolic pathway is demethylation, by CYP2C19, with subsequent sulfation; other metabolic pathways include oxidation by CYP3A4. There is no evidence that any of the pantoprazole metabolites have significant pharmacologic activity."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00214"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"348.1256"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Metabolized via the hepatic CYP2C8 to 5 metabolites. The major metabolite, M5, is pharmacologically inactive. There are 2 minor metabolites, M1, possessing one-tenth the activity of torasemide, and M3, equal in activity to torasemide. Overall, torasemide appears to account for 80% of the total diuretic activity, while metabolites M1 and M3 account for 9% and 11%, respectively."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00215"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"324.1638"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00215"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"324.1638"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00216"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"382.1715"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"In vitro studies indicate that eletriptan is primarily metabolized by cytochrome P-450 enzyme CYP3A4. The N-demethylated metabolite of eletriptan is the only known active metabolite."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00217"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"388.0907"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00219"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"427.1722"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00221"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"239.1521"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00221"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"239.1521"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00222"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"490.225"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic. Following either an intravenous or oral dose, glimepiride is completely metabolized by oxidative biotransformation to a major metabolite, cyclohexyl hydroxymethyl derivative (M1), via the hepatic cytochrome P450 II C9 subsystem. M1 is further metabolized to the carboxyl derivative (M2) by one or several cytosolic enzymes. M1, but not M2, possessed approximately one third of the pharmacologic activity of its parent in an animal model. However, whether the glucose-lowering effect of M1 is clinically significant is not clear."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00222"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"490.225"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic. Following either an intravenous or oral dose, glimepiride is completely metabolized by oxidative biotransformation to a major metabolite, cyclohexyl hydroxymethyl derivative (M1), via the hepatic cytochrome P450 II C9 subsystem. M1 is further metabolized to the carboxyl derivative (M2) by one or several cytosolic enzymes. M1, but not M2, possessed approximately one third of the pharmacologic activity of its parent in an animal model. However, whether the glucose-lowering effect of M1 is clinically significant is not clear."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00379"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"179.131"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Primarily hepatic (85%) via CYP2D6 and CYP1A2 (primarily CYP2D6)."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00379"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"179.131"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Primarily hepatic (85%) via CYP2D6 and CYP1A2 (primarily CYP2D6)."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00380"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"268.1172"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Dexrazoxane is hydrolysed by the enzyme dihydropyrimidine amidohydrolase in the liver and kidney to active metabolites that are capable of binding to metal ions."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00381"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"408.1452"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00381"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"408.1452"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00382"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"198.1157"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic. Cytochrome P450 1A2 is the principal isozyme involved in tacrine metabolism. The major metabolite, 1-hydroxy-tacrine (velnacrine), has central cholinergic activity."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00382"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"198.1157"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic. Cytochrome P450 1A2 is the principal isozyme involved in tacrine metabolism. The major metabolite, 1-hydroxy-tacrine (velnacrine), has central cholinergic activity."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00383"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"344.21"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00383"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"344.21"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00384"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"253.1076"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Triamterene is primarily metabolized to the sulfate conjugate of hydroxytriamterene. Both the plasma and urine levels of this metabolite greatly exceed triamterene levels."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00385"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"723.2139"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Valrubicin is metabolized to two primary metabolites: N-trifluoroacetyladriamycin and N-trifluoroacetyladriamycinol."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00387"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"287.2249"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00387"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"287.2249"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00388"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"167.0946"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Oral phenylephrine is extensively metabolised by monoamine oxidase, an enzyme which is present in the stomach and liver."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00388"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"167.0946"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Oral phenylephrine is extensively metabolised by monoamine oxidase, an enzyme which is present in the stomach and liver."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00171"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"506.9957"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00172"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"115.0633"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00172"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"115.0633"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00173"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"135.0545"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00173"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"135.0545"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00106"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"1414.6841"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"\u003ci\u003eIn vitro\u003c/i\u003e hepatocyte (rat, monkey, human) studies and \u003ci\u003ein vivo\u003c/i\u003e studies in rats and monkeys showed that the major metabolites of abarelix were formed via hydrolysis of peptide bonds. No significant oxidative or conjugated metabolites of abarelix were found either \u003ci\u003ein vitro\u003c/i\u003e or \u003ci\u003ein vivo\u003c/i\u003e. There is no evidence of cytochrome P-450 involvement in the metabolism of abarelix."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00107"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"1006.4365"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00107"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"1006.4365"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00111"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"144031.1268"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Most likely removed by opsonization via the reticuloendothelial system when bound to lymphocytes."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00112"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"149104.8407"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Most likely removed by opsonization via the reticuloendothelial system when bound to endothelial cells, or by human antimurine antibody production"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00114"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"247.0246"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00115"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"1354.5674"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00185"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"199.1031"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Primarily hepatic, isozymes CYP2D6 and CYP3A4 are responsible for the metabolism of cevimeline. Approximately 44.5% of the drug is converted to cis and trans-sulfoxide, 22.3% to glucuronic acid conjugate, and 4% to N-oxide of cevimeline. Approximately 8% of the trans-sulfoxide metabolite is then converted into the corresponding glucuronic acid conjugate."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00354"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"432.2332"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00354"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"432.2332"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00356"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"168.9931"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Chlorzoxazone is rapidly metabolized in the liver and is excreted in the urine, primarily in a conjugated form as the glucuronide."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00223"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"410.1905"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Metabolized, primarily in the liver, and then excreted by the kidneys."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00345"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"216.0511"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00346"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"389.2063"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic. Alfuzosin undergoes extensive metabolism by the liver, with only 11% of the administered dose excreted unchanged in the urine. Alfuzosin is metabolized by three metabolic pathways: oxidation, O-demethylations, and N-dealkylation. The metabolites are not pharmacologically active. CYP3A4 is the principal hepatic enzyme isoform involved in its metabolism."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00347"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"143.0582"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00348"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"329.0511"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00349"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"300.0666"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic. Clobazam has two major metabolites: N-desmethyl-clobazam and 4'-hydroxyclobazam, the former of which is active. The demethylation is facilitated by CYP2C19, CYP3A4, and CYP2B6 and the 4'-hydroxyclobazam by CYP2C18 and CYP2C19."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00350"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"209.1277"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Approximately 90% of the administered drug is metabolized, predominantly by conjugation with glucuronic acid at the N-oxide position in the pyrimidine ring, but also by conversion to more polar products. Known metabolites exert much less pharmacologic effect than minoxidil itself."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00351"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"342.2195"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Primarily hepatic. Megestrol metabolites which were identified in urine constituted 5% to 8% of the dose administered. Respiratory excretion as labeled carbon dioxide and fat storage may have accounted for at least part of the radioactivity not found in urine and feces. No active metabolites have been identified."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00351"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"342.2195"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Primarily hepatic. Megestrol metabolites which were identified in urine constituted 5% to 8% of the dose administered. Respiratory excretion as labeled carbon dioxide and fat storage may have accounted for at least part of the radioactivity not found in urine and feces. No active metabolites have been identified."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00352"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"167.0266"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic. First converted to 6-thioguanilyic acid (TGMP). TGMP is further converted to the di- and tri-phosphates, thioguanosine diphosphate (TGDP) and thioguanosine triphosphate (TGTP) by the same enzymes that metabolize guanine nucleotides."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00352"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"167.0266"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic. First converted to 6-thioguanilyic acid (TGMP). TGMP is further converted to the di- and tri-phosphates, thioguanosine diphosphate (TGDP) and thioguanosine triphosphate (TGTP) by the same enzymes that metabolize guanine nucleotides."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00444"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"656.1564"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00445"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"543.1741"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00445"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"543.1741"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00448"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"369.0759"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic. Two metabolites have been identified in measurable quantities in plasma (the hydroxylated sulfinyl and sulfone derivatives of lansoprazole). These metabolites have very little or no antisecretory activity. Lansoprazole is thought to be transformed into two active species which inhibit acid secretion by (H\u003csup\u003e+\u003c/sup\u003e,K\u003csup\u003e+\u003c/sup\u003e)-ATPase within the parietal cell canaliculus, but are not present in the systemic circulation."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00449"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"351.2046"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Dipivefrin is converted to epinephrine inside the human eye by enzyme hydrolysis."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00449"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"351.2046"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Dipivefrin is converted to epinephrine inside the human eye by enzyme hydrolysis."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00450"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"379.1696"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Extensively metabolized."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00451"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"776.6867"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic (primarily via cytochrome P450 isozyme 3A4)."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00454"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"247.1572"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Meperidine is metabolized in the liver by hydrolysis to meperidinic acid followed by partial conjugation with glucuronic acid. Meperidine also undergoes N-demethylation to normeperidine, which then undergoes hydrolysis and partial conjugation. Normeperidine is about half as potent as meperidine, but it has twice the CNS stimulation effects."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00454"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"247.1572"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Meperidine is metabolized in the liver by hydrolysis to meperidinic acid followed by partial conjugation with glucuronic acid. Meperidine also undergoes N-demethylation to normeperidine, which then undergoes hydrolysis and partial conjugation. Normeperidine is about half as potent as meperidine, but it has twice the CNS stimulation effects."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00455"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"382.1448"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00457"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"383.1594"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Primarily hepatic. Several metabolites have been identified in humans and animals (6- O -demethyl, 7- O -demethyl, 2-[1-piperazinyl]-4-amino-6, 7-dimethoxyquinazoline, 2,4-diamino-6,7-dimethoxyquinazoline)."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00457"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"383.1594"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Primarily hepatic. Several metabolites have been identified in humans and animals (6- O -demethyl, 7- O -demethyl, 2-[1-piperazinyl]-4-amino-6, 7-dimethoxyquinazoline, 2,4-diamino-6,7-dimethoxyquinazoline)."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00458"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"280.1939"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Exclusively hepatic. Imipramine is converted in the liver to desipramine and 2-hydroxydesipramine, both active metabolites."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00458"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"280.1939"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Exclusively hepatic. Imipramine is converted in the liver to desipramine and 2-hydroxydesipramine, both active metabolites."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00323"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"273.0637"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"The main metabolic pathway of tolcapone is glucuronidation"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00324"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"376.205"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00325"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"215.9483"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Metabolized by reaction with hemoglobin to produce cyanmethemoglobin and cynide ions"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00326"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"490.0847"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00327"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"285.1365"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Primarily hepatic. After absorption hydromorphone is metabolized by the liver to the glucuronide conjugate which is then excreted in the urine. Hydromorphone is metabolized to the major metabolites hydromorphone-3-glucuronide, hydromorphone-3-glucoside and dihydroisomorphine-6-glucuronide."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00327"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"285.1365"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Primarily hepatic. After absorption hydromorphone is metabolized by the liver to the glucuronide conjugate which is then excreted in the urine. Hydromorphone is metabolized to the major metabolites hydromorphone-3-glucuronide, hydromorphone-3-glucoside and dihydroisomorphine-6-glucuronide."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00328"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"357.0768"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00328"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"357.0768"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00330"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"204.1838"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic. Up to 15% of administered drug is metabolized to inactive metabolites. The main path of metabolism appears to be an initial oxidation of the alcohol to an aldehydic intermediate, followed by conversion to a dicarboxylic acid."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00330"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"204.1838"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic. Up to 15% of administered drug is metabolized to inactive metabolites. The main path of metabolism appears to be an initial oxidation of the alcohol to an aldehydic intermediate, followed by conversion to a dicarboxylic acid."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00331"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"129.1014"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Metformin is not metabolized."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00331"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"129.1014"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Metformin is not metabolized."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00332"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"332.2226"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00333"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"309.2093"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic. Cytochrome P450 enzymes, primarily CYP3A4, CYP2B6, and CYP2C19 and to a lesser extent CYP2C9 and CYP2D6, are responsible for conversion of methadone to EDDP and other inactive metabolites, which are excreted mainly in the urine."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00333"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"309.2093"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic. Cytochrome P450 enzymes, primarily CYP3A4, CYP2B6, and CYP2C19 and to a lesser extent CYP2C9 and CYP2D6, are responsible for conversion of methadone to EDDP and other inactive metabolites, which are excreted mainly in the urine."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00227"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"404.2563"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00227"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"404.2563"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00228"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"183.9714"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"2.4% of the dose is slowly metabolized hepatically via oxidation and dehalogenation (primarily through the actions of cytochrome P450 2E1). Leads to low levels of serum fluoride (15 \u0026micro;mol/L)."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00228"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"183.9714"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"2.4% of the dose is slowly metabolized hepatically via oxidation and dehalogenation (primarily through the actions of cytochrome P450 2E1). Leads to low levels of serum fluoride (15 \u0026micro;mol/L)."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00230"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"159.1259"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Negligible"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00231"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"300.0666"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic. Temazepam is completely metabolized through conjugation prior to excretion. The major metabolite is the O-conjugate of temazepam (90%)."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00232"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"358.9568"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00232"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"358.9568"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00235"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"211.0746"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"There are five metabolites but the O-glucuronide represents the major pathway of biotransformation."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00235"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"211.0746"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"There are five metabolites but the O-glucuronide represents the major pathway of biotransformation."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00236"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"353.9579"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00236"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"353.9579"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00237"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"212.1161"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00237"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"212.1161"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00240"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"520.2228"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00459"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"326.1882"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Following oral absorption, acitretin undergoes extensive metabolism and interconversion by simple isomerization to its 13-cis form (cis-acitretin). Both parent compound and isomer are further metabolized into chain-shortened breakdown products and conjugates, which are excreted."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00461"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"228.115"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Undergoes rapid biotransformation to the principal active metabolite, 6-methoxy-2-naphthylacetic acid (6MNA). Approximately 35% of a 1000 mg oral dose of nabumetone is converted to 6MNA and 50% is converted into unidentified metabolites which are subsequently excreted in the urine."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00462"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"397.0889"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Little is known about the fate and excretion of methscopolamine."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00463"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"198.1004"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00468"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"324.1838"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic, over 80% metabolized by the liver."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00468"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"324.1838"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic, over 80% metabolized by the liver."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00401"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"388.1634"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Pre-systemic metabolism in the gut wall, and this metabolism decreases from the proximal to the distal parts of the intestine. Nisoldipine is highly metabolized; 5 major urinary metabolites have been identified. The major biotransformation pathway appears to be the hydroxylation of the isobutyl ester. A hydroxylated derivative of the side chain, present in plasma at concentrations approximately equal to the parent compound, appears to be the only active metabolite and has about 10% of the activity of the parent compound. Cytochrome P450 enzymes are believed to play a major role in the metabolism of nisoldipine. The particular isoenzyme system responsible for its metabolism has not been identified, but other dihydropyridines are metabolized by cytochrome P450 IIIA4."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00401"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"388.1634"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Pre-systemic metabolism in the gut wall, and this metabolism decreases from the proximal to the distal parts of the intestine. Nisoldipine is highly metabolized; 5 major urinary metabolites have been identified. The major biotransformation pathway appears to be the hydroxylation of the isobutyl ester. A hydroxylated derivative of the side chain, present in plasma at concentrations approximately equal to the parent compound, appears to be the only active metabolite and has about 10% of the activity of the parent compound. Cytochrome P450 enzymes are believed to play a major role in the metabolism of nisoldipine. The particular isoenzyme system responsible for its metabolism has not been identified, but other dihydropyridines are metabolized by cytochrome P450 IIIA4."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00402"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"388.1051"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Following oral administration, eszopiclone is extensively metabolized by oxidation and demethylation."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00403"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"1351.4485"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00404"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"308.0829"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic. Hydroxylated in the liver to \u0026alpha;-hydroxyalprazolam, which is also active. This and other metabolites are later excreted in urine as glucuronides."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00404"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"308.0829"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic. Hydroxylated in the liver to \u0026alpha;-hydroxyalprazolam, which is also active. This and other metabolites are later excreted in urine as glucuronides."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00376"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"301.2406"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00376"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"301.2406"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00378"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"312.2089"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Metabolism is complete to a 20-dihydrodydrogesterone (DHD) metabolite."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00284"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"645.248"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00284"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"645.248"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00321"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"277.1831"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Exclusively hepatic, with first pass effect. Amitriptyline is demethylated in the liver to its primary active metabolite, nortriptyline."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00321"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"277.1831"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Exclusively hepatic, with first pass effect. Amitriptyline is demethylated in the liver to its primary active metabolite, nortriptyline."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00322"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"246.0652"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00322"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"246.0652"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00323"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"273.0637"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"The main metabolic pathway of tolcapone is glucuronidation"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00275"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"558.2227"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Olmesartan is rapidly and completely bioactivated by ester hydrolysis to olmesartan during absorption from the gastrointestinal tract. There is virtually no further metabolism of olmesartan."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00276"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"393.1147"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Extensive, primarily hepatic, converted to glutathione conjugate."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00276"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"393.1147"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Extensive, primarily hepatic, converted to glutathione conjugate."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00277"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"180.0647"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic. Biotransformation takes place through demethylation to 1-methylxanthine and 3-methylxanthine and hydroxylation to 1,3-dimethyluric acid. 1-methylxanthine is further hydroxylated, by xanthine oxidase, to 1-methyluric acid. About 6% of a theophylline dose is N-methylated to caffeine. Caffeine and 3-methylxanthine are the only theophylline metabolites with pharmacologic activity."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00277"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"180.0647"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic. Biotransformation takes place through demethylation to 1-methylxanthine and 3-methylxanthine and hydroxylation to 1,3-dimethyluric acid. 1-methylxanthine is further hydroxylated, by xanthine oxidase, to 1-methyluric acid. About 6% of a theophylline dose is N-methylated to caffeine. Caffeine and 3-methylxanthine are the only theophylline metabolites with pharmacologic activity."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00278"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"508.2468"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00279"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"650.79"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00280"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"339.2311"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00280"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"339.2311"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00281"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"234.1732"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Primarily hepatic."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00281"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"234.1732"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Primarily hepatic."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00282"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"235.0011"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Pamidronate is not metabolized and is exclusively eliminated by renal excretion"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00282"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"235.0011"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Pamidronate is not metabolized and is exclusively eliminated by renal excretion"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00283"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"343.1703"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Antihistamines appear to be metabolized in the liver chiefly via mono- and didemethylation and glucuronide conjugation."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00283"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"343.1703"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Antihistamines appear to be metabolized in the liver chiefly via mono- and didemethylation and glucuronide conjugation."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00304"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"310.2297"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Desogestrel is rapidly and completely metabolized by hydroxylation in the intestinal mucosa and on first pass through the liver to etonogestrel. Other metabolites (i.e., 3a-OH-desogestrel, 3b-OH-desogestrel, and 3a-OH-5a-H-desogestrel) with no pharmacologic actions also have been identified and these metabolites may undergo glucuronide and sulfate conjugation."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00304"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"310.2297"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Desogestrel is rapidly and completely metabolized by hydroxylation in the intestinal mucosa and on first pass through the liver to etonogestrel. Other metabolites (i.e., 3a-OH-desogestrel, 3b-OH-desogestrel, and 3a-OH-5a-H-desogestrel) with no pharmacologic actions also have been identified and these metabolites may undergo glucuronide and sulfate conjugation."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00305"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"334.1277"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Primarily hepatic, some in various other tissues."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00305"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"334.1277"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Primarily hepatic, some in various other tissues."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00306"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"224.1161"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00307"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"348.2089"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00308"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"884.5003"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Primarily hepatic. Eight metabolites of ibutilide were detected in metabolic profiling of urine. These metabolites are thought to be formed primarily by o-oxidation followed by sequential b-oxidation of the heptyl side chain of ibutilide. Of the eight metabolites, only the o-hydroxy metabolite possesses class III electrophysiologic properties similar to that of ibutilide in an in vitro isolated rabbit myocardium model."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00309"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"851.3775"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00310"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"338.0128"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Liver"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00311"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"258.0133"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00312"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"226.1317"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"by hepatic microsomal enzyme system"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00312"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"226.1317"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"by hepatic microsomal enzyme system"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00313"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"144.115"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Valproic Acid is metabolized almost entirely by the liver. In adult patients on monotherapy, 30-50% of an administered dose appears in urine as a glucuronide conjugate. Mitochondrial \u0026szlig;-oxidation is the other major metabolic pathway, typically accounting for over 40% of the dose. Usually, less than 15-20% of the dose is eliminated by other oxidative mechanisms. Less than 3% of an administered dose is excreted unchanged in urine."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00313"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"144.115"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Valproic Acid is metabolized almost entirely by the liver. In adult patients on monotherapy, 30-50% of an administered dose appears in urine as a glucuronide conjugate. Mitochondrial \u0026szlig;-oxidation is the other major metabolic pathway, typically accounting for over 40% of the dose. Usually, less than 15-20% of the dose is eliminated by other oxidative mechanisms. Less than 3% of an administered dose is excreted unchanged in urine."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00389"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"186.0463"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00389"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"186.0463"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00297"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"288.2202"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Amide-type local anesthetics such as bupivacaine are metabolized primarily in the liver via conjugation with glucuronic acid. The major metabolite of bupivacaine is 2,6-pipecoloxylidine, which is mainly catalyzed via cytochrome P450 3A4."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00298"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"325.2266"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00302"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"157.1103"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Only a small fraction of the drug is metabolized (less than 5%)."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00469"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"337.0191"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Tenoxicam is metabolized in the liver to several pharmacologically inactive metabolites (mainly 5'-hydroxy-tenoxicam)."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00470"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"314.2246"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00470"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"314.2246"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00471"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"585.2104"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00334"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"312.1409"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00334"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"312.1409"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00335"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"266.163"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic (minimal)"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00337"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"809.4481"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Metabolized by the CYP3A sub-family of metabolizing enzymes."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00428"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"265.091"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Primarily hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00428"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"265.091"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Primarily hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00429"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"489.3302"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Metabolized in the lungs and liver. Metabolites are excreted in urine."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00193"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"263.1885"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"The major metabolic pathways appear to be N- and O- demethylation and glucuronidation or sulfation in the liver. One metabolite (O-desmethyltramadol, denoted M1) is pharmacologically active in animal models."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00195"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"307.2147"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Primarily hepatic. Approximately 15% of the dose administered is excreted as unchanged drug, the remainder being metabolites whose contribution to the clinical effect is negligible."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00195"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"307.2147"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Primarily hepatic. Approximately 15% of the dose administered is excreted as unchanged drug, the remainder being metabolites whose contribution to the clinical effect is negligible."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00197"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"441.161"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"A sulfate conjugate metabolite (Metabolite 1) and a quinone metabolite (Metabolite 3) have been detected in the plasma of healthy males. A glucuronide conjugate (Metabolite 2) has been detected in the urine and also in negligible amounts in the plasma. In healthy volunteers and in patients with type 2 diabetes, the steady-state concentration of Metabolite 1 is six to seven times that of troglitazone and Metabolite 3. In in vivo drug interaction studies, troglitazone has been shown to induce cytochrome P450 CYP3A4 at clinically relevant doses."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00200"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"1345.5671"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Primarily hepatic. Cobalamins are absorbed in the ileum and stored in the liver. They continuously undergo enterohepatic recycling via secretion in the bile. Part of a dose is excreted in the urine, most of it in the first 8 hours."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00200"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"1345.5671"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Primarily hepatic. Cobalamins are absorbed in the ileum and stored in the liver. They continuously undergo enterohepatic recycling via secretion in the bile. Part of a dose is excreted in the urine, most of it in the first 8 hours."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00201"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"194.0804"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic cytochrome P450 1A2 (CYP 1A2) is involved in caffeine biotransformation. About 80% of a dose of caffeine is metabolized to paraxanthine (1,7-dimethylxanthine), 10% to theobromine (3,7-dimethylxanthine), and 4% to theophylline (1,3-dimethylxanthine)."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00201"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"194.0804"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic cytochrome P450 1A2 (CYP 1A2) is involved in caffeine biotransformation. About 80% of a dose of caffeine is metabolized to paraxanthine (1,7-dimethylxanthine), 10% to theobromine (3,7-dimethylxanthine), and 4% to theophylline (1,3-dimethylxanthine)."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00202"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"290.2206"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"By pseudocholinesterase, to succinylmonocholine and choline."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00203"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"474.2049"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00203"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"474.2049"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00204"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"441.1392"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00204"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"441.1392"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00162"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"286.2297"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic. Retinol is conjugated with glucuronic acid; the B-glucuronide undergoes enterohepatic circulation and oxidation to retinol and retinoic acid. Retinoic acid undergoes decarboxylation and conjugation with glucuronic acid."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00163"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"430.3811"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00165"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"169.0739"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00165"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"169.0739"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00166"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"206.0435"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00166"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"206.0435"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00167"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"131.0946"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00167"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"131.0946"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00168"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"294.1216"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Approximately 10% of aspartame (by weight) is broken down into methanol in the small intestine. Most of the methanol is absorbed and quickly converted into formaldehyde. Approximately 50% of aspartame (by weight) is broken down into phenylalanine. Approximately 40% of aspartame (by mass) is broken down into aspartic acid."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00168"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"294.1216"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Approximately 10% of aspartame (by weight) is broken down into methanol in the small intestine. Most of the methanol is absorbed and quickly converted into formaldehyde. Approximately 50% of aspartame (by weight) is broken down into phenylalanine. Approximately 40% of aspartame (by mass) is broken down into aspartic acid."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00169"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"384.3392"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Within the liver, cholecalciferal is hydroxylated to calcidiol (25-hydroxycholecalciferol) by the enzyme 25-hydroxylase. Within the kidney, calcidiol serves as a substrate for 1-alpha-hydroxylase, yielding calcitriol (1,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol), the biologically active form of vitamin D3."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00169"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"384.3392"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Within the liver, cholecalciferal is hydroxylated to calcidiol (25-hydroxycholecalciferol) by the enzyme 25-hydroxylase. Within the kidney, calcidiol serves as a substrate for 1-alpha-hydroxylase, yielding calcitriol (1,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol), the biologically active form of vitamin D3."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00170"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"172.0524"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00170"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"172.0524"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00105"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"19256.7431"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00269"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"380.1179"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Metabolized principally in the liver, although the kidneys, gonads, and muscle tissues may be involved to some extent. The metabolic fate of the synthetic estrogens has not been fully elucidated."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00270"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"371.1481"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic. Completely metabolized prior to excretion and no unchanged drug is detected in the urine."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00271"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"613.7696"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00273"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"339.0988"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Not extensively metabolized, 70% of the dose is eliminated unchanged in the urine. The other 30% is metabolized hepatically to six metabolites (formed by hydroxylation, hydrolysis, and glucuronidation), none of which constitute more than 5% of an administered dose."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00273"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"339.0988"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Not extensively metabolized, 70% of the dose is eliminated unchanged in the urine. The other 30% is metabolized hepatically to six metabolites (formed by hydroxylation, hydrolysis, and glucuronidation), none of which constitute more than 5% of an administered dose."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00802"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"416.2536"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"The liver is the major site of biotransformation."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00802"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"416.2536"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"The liver is the major site of biotransformation."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","