(dFdU)."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00441"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"263.0718"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Transformed via nucleoside kinases to two active metabolites, gemcitabine diphosphate and gemcitabine triphosphate. Can also undergo deamination via cytidine deaminase to an inactive uracil metabolite (dFdU)."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00443"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"392.1999"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00444"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"656.1564"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00517"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"361.1616"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic, by enzymatic hydrolysis."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00517"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"361.1616"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic, by enzymatic hydrolysis."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00519"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"430.2468"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Cleavage of the ester group of trandolapril, primarily in the liver, is responsible for conversion to trandolaprilat, the active metabolite. Seven other metabolites, resulting primarily from glucuronidation or de-esterification, have been identified."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00521"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"292.1787"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00521"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"292.1787"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00522"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"404.1372"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Primarily hepatic. Enzymatic activity capable of hydrolyzing bentiromide has also been found in normal small intestine."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00523"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"300.2089"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00523"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"300.2089"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00524"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"365.0601"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00526"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"397.0601"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Oxaliplatin undergoes nonenzymatic conversion in physiologic solutions to active derivatives via displacement of the labile oxalate ligand. Several transient reactive species are formed, including monoaquo and diaquo DACH platinum, which covalently bind with macromolecules. There is no evidence of cytochrome P450-mediated metabolism in vitro."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00527"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"343.226"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Primarily hepatic."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00527"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"343.226"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Primarily hepatic."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00530"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"393.1689"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"In vitro assays of cytochrome P450 metabolism showed that erlotinib is metabolized primarily by CYP3A4 and to a lesser extent by CYP1A2, and the extrahepatic isoform CYP1A1."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00531"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"278.0354"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00532"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"218.1055"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00533"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"314.0613"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic. Metabolism of rofecoxib is primarily mediated through reduction by cytosolic enzymes. The principal metabolic products are the cis-dihydro and trans-dihydro derivatives of rofecoxib, which account for nearly 56% of recovered radioactivity in the urine. An additional 8.8% of the dose was recovered as the glucuronide of the hydroxy derivative, a product of oxidative metabolism. The biotransformation of rofecoxib and this metabolite is reversible in humans to a limited extent (\u0026lt; 5%). These metabolites are inactive as COX-1 or COX-2 inhibitors. Cytochrome P450 plays a minor role in metabolism of rofecoxib."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00533"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"314.0613"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic. Metabolism of rofecoxib is primarily mediated through reduction by cytosolic enzymes. The principal metabolic products are the cis-dihydro and trans-dihydro derivatives of rofecoxib, which account for nearly 56% of recovered radioactivity in the urine. An additional 8.8% of the dose was recovered as the glucuronide of the hydroxy derivative, a product of oxidative metabolism. The biotransformation of rofecoxib and this metabolite is reversible in humans to a limited extent (\u0026lt; 5%). These metabolites are inactive as COX-1 or COX-2 inhibitors. Cytochrome P450 plays a minor role in metabolism of rofecoxib."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00538"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"662.1547"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"None detected"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00539"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"405.1859"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic. Mainly by CYP3A4."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00539"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"405.1859"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic. Mainly by CYP3A4."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00540"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"263.1674"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00540"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"263.1674"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00541"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"824.3996"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00541"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"824.3996"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00542"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"424.1998"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Cleavage of the ester group (primarily in the liver) converts benazepril to its active metabolite, benazeprilat."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00542"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"424.1998"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Cleavage of the ester group (primarily in the liver) converts benazepril to its active metabolite, benazeprilat."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00543"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"313.0982"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Amoxapine is almost completely metabolized, producing the major metabolite 8-hydroxyamoxapine."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00408"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"327.1138"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00408"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"327.1138"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00409"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"370.0892"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"No active metabolites"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00411"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"182.0822"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00413"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"211.1143"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"No metabolites have been identified in plasma or urine."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00414"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"324.1144"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Extensively metabolized in the liver to the active metabolite hydroxyhexamide, which exhibits greater hypoglycemic potency than acetohexamide. Hydroxyhexamide is believed to be responsible for prolonged hypoglycemic effects."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00414"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"324.1144"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Extensively metabolized in the liver to the active metabolite hydroxyhexamide, which exhibits greater hypoglycemic potency than acetohexamide. Hydroxyhexamide is believed to be responsible for prolonged hypoglycemic effects."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00285"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"277.2042"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00285"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"277.2042"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00286"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"372.1007"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00471"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"585.2104"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00472"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"309.134"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00473"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"261.1729"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hydrolyzed by plasma esterases to benzoic acid and other derivatives"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00474"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"262.1317"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Metabolism occurs in the liver through demethylation and oxidation. Side-chain oxidation is the most important biotransformation involved in termination of biologic activity."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00474"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"262.1317"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Metabolism occurs in the liver through demethylation and oxidation. Side-chain oxidation is the most important biotransformation involved in termination of biologic activity."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00475"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"299.0825"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00476"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"333.0954"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"The major biotransformation pathways for duloxetine involve oxidation of the naphthyl ring followed by conjugation and further oxidation. Both CYP2D6 and CYP1A2 catalyze the oxidation of the naphthyl ring in vitro. Metabolites found in plasma include 4-hydroxy duloxetine glucuronide and 5-hydroxy, 6-methoxy duloxetine sulfate. The major circulating metabolites have not been shown to contribute significantly to the pharmacologic activity of duloxetine."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00477"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"318.0957"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00477"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"318.0957"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00480"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"259.0957"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"The metabolic profile of lenalidomide in humans has not been studied. In healthy volunteers, approximately two-thirds of lenalidomide is eliminated unchanged through urinary excretion. The process exceeds the glomerular filtration rate and therefore is partially or entirely active."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00481"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"473.1661"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic, raloxifene undergoes extensive first-pass metabolism to the glucuronide conjugates: raloxifene-4'-glucuronide, raloxifene-6-glucuronide, and raloxifene-6, 4'-diglucuronide. No other metabolites have been detected, providing strong evidence that raloxifene is not metabolized by cytochrome P450 pathways"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00481"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"473.1661"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic, raloxifene undergoes extensive first-pass metabolism to the glucuronide conjugates: raloxifene-4'-glucuronide, raloxifene-6-glucuronide, and raloxifene-6, 4'-diglucuronide. No other metabolites have been detected, providing strong evidence that raloxifene is not metabolized by cytochrome P450 pathways"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00482"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"381.0759"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic. Celecoxib metabolism is primarily mediated via cytochrome P450 2C9. Three metabolites, a primary alcohol, the corresponding carboxylic acid and its glucuronide conjugate, have been identified in human plasma. These metabolites are inactive as COX-1 or COX-2 inhibitors."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00482"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"381.0759"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic. Celecoxib metabolism is primarily mediated via cytochrome P450 2C9. Three metabolites, a primary alcohol, the corresponding carboxylic acid and its glucuronide conjugate, have been identified in human plasma. These metabolites are inactive as COX-1 or COX-2 inhibitors."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00483"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"891.1769"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00287"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"500.2386"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Travoprost, an isopropyl ester prodrug, is hydrolyzed by esterases in the cornea to its biologically active free acid. Systemically, travoprost free acid is metabolized to inactive metabolites via beta-oxidation of the \u0026alpha;(carboxylic acid) chain to give the 1,2-dinor and 1,2,3,4-tetranor analogs, via oxidation of the 15-hydroxyl moiety, as well as via reduction of the 13,14 double bond."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00288"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"502.2367"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Once absorbed through the skin, topical corticosteroids are handled through pharmacokinetic pathways similar to systemically administered corticosteroids. Corticosteroids are metabolized primarily in the liver and are then excreted by the kidneys."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00289"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"291.139"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Atomoxetine is primarily metabolized by the CYP2D6 pathway to 4-hydroxyatomoxetine. 4-Hydroxyatomoxetine is equipotent to atomoxetine as an inhibitor of the norepinephrine transporter but circulates in plasma at much lower concentrations (1% of atomoxetine concentration in EMs and 0.1% of atomoxetine concentration in PMs)."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00291"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"303.0793"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00292"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"244.1212"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic. Metabolized rapidly by ester hydrolysis to inactive metabolites."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00292"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"244.1212"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic. Metabolized rapidly by ester hydrolysis to inactive metabolites."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00293"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"458.126"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Raltitrexed is transported into cells via a reduced folate carrier. Inside the cell it is extensively polyglutamated by the enzyme folyl polyglutamate synthetase to polyglutamate forms."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00293"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"458.126"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Raltitrexed is transported into cells via a reduced folate carrier. Inside the cell it is extensively polyglutamated by the enzyme folyl polyglutamate synthetase to polyglutamate forms."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00294"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"324.2089"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00295"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"285.1365"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Primarily hepatic (90%), converted to dihydromorphinone and normorphine. Also converted to morphine-3-glucuronide (M3G) and morphine-6-glucuronide. Virtually all morphine is converted to glucuronide metabolites; only a small fraction (less than 5%) of absorbed morphine is demethylated."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00295"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"285.1365"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Primarily hepatic (90%), converted to dihydromorphinone and normorphine. Also converted to morphine-3-glucuronide (M3G) and morphine-6-glucuronide. Virtually all morphine is converted to glucuronide metabolites; only a small fraction (less than 5%) of absorbed morphine is demethylated."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00296"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"274.2045"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00296"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"274.2045"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00563"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"454.1713"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00563"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"454.1713"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00564"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"236.095"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00564"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"236.095"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00565"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"1242.5004"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"The degradation of cisatracurium is largely independent of liver metabolism. Results from in vitro experiments suggest that cisatracurium undergoes Hofmann elimination (a pH and temperature-dependent chemical process) to form laudanosine and the monoquaternary acrylate metabolite. The monoquaternary acrylate undergoes hydrolysis by non-specific plasma esterases to form the monoquaternary alcohol metabolite. The monoquaternary alcohol metabolite can also undergo Hofmann elimination but at a much slower rate than cisatracurium. Laudanosine is further metabolized to desmethyl metabolites which are conjugated with glucuronic acid and excreted in the urine."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00566"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"181.9708"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Chemical analysis of succimer and its metabolites (primarily mixed disulfides of L-cysteine) in the urine showed that succimer was rapidly and extensively metabolized however the specific site of biotransformation is not known."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00566"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"181.9708"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Chemical analysis of succimer and its metabolites (primarily mixed disulfides of L-cysteine) in the urine showed that succimer was rapidly and extensively metabolized however the specific site of biotransformation is not known."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00568"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"368.2252"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00569"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"1726.7708"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00570"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"810.4204"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic. Metabolism of vinblastine has been shown to be mediated by hepatic cytochrome P450 3A isoenzymes."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00570"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"810.4204"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic. Metabolism of vinblastine has been shown to be mediated by hepatic cytochrome P450 3A isoenzymes."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00571"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"259.1572"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00571"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"259.1572"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00572"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"289.1678"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Much of the drug is destroyed by enzymatic hydrolysis, particularly in the liver. From 13 to 50% is excreted unchanged in the urine."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00572"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"289.1678"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Much of the drug is destroyed by enzymatic hydrolysis, particularly in the liver. From 13 to 50% is excreted unchanged in the urine."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00574"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"231.1235"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00574"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"231.1235"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00575"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"229.0174"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic. Metabolized through minor pathways. The major metabolite, p-hydroxyclonidine, is present in concentrations less than 10% of those of unchanged clonidine in urine."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00579"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"284.0716"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00580"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"314.0725"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic (involves CYP3A4 and 2C9)"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00581"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"342.1162"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Lactulose is completely metabolized in the colon by enteric bacteria, and no lactulose is excreted in the feces."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00581"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"342.1162"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Lactulose is completely metabolized in the colon by enteric bacteria, and no lactulose is excreted in the feces."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00645"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"289.2042"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00645"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"289.2042"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00647"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"339.2198"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00647"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"339.2198"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00648"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"317.9537"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic and renal"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00650"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"511.1128"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic and intestinal mucosal, the main metabolite being the active 5-methyltetrahydrofolate. Leucovorin is readily converted to another reduced folate, 5,10-methylenetetrahydrofolate, which acts to stabilize the binding of fluorodeoxyridylic acid to thymidylate synthase and thereby enhances the inhibition of this enzyme."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00651"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"254.1015"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00484"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"291.012"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Metabolized primarily by the liver."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00484"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"291.012"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Metabolized primarily by the liver."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00486"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"372.2664"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic. Two metabolic pathways have been suggested. The major pathway probably involves the direct oxidation of Nabilone to produce hydroxylic and carboxylic analogues. These compounds are thought to account for the remaining plasma radioactivity when carbinol metabolites have been extracted."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00488"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"210.1593"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00489"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"272.1195"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Sotalol is not metabolized."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00489"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"272.1195"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Sotalol is not metabolized."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00490"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"385.2478"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Metabolized hepatically, primarily by oxidation by cytochrome P450 3A4 producing several hydroxylated derivatives and a pharmacologically active metabolite, 1-pyrimidinylpiperazine (1-PP)"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00490"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"385.2478"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Metabolized hepatically, primarily by oxidation by cytochrome P450 3A4 producing several hydroxylated derivatives and a pharmacologically active metabolite, 1-pyrimidinylpiperazine (1-PP)"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00491"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"207.1107"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Miglitol is not metabolized in man or in any animal species studied."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00491"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"207.1107"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Miglitol is not metabolized in man or in any animal species studied."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00492"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"563.3012"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Since fosinoprilat is not biotransformed after intravenous administration, fosinopril, not fosinoprilat, appears to be the precursor for the glucuronide and p-hydroxy metabolites."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00492"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"563.3012"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Since fosinoprilat is not biotransformed after intravenous administration, fosinopril, not fosinoprilat, appears to be the precursor for the glucuronide and p-hydroxy metabolites."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00494"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"305.1012"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Metabolized via isomerization to the cis-isomer, followed by direct glucuronidation of the parent and cis-isomer."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00494"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"305.1012"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Metabolized via isomerization to the cis-isomer, followed by direct glucuronidation of the parent and cis-isomer."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00496"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"426.2307"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic. Primarily mediated by the cytochrome P450 enzymes CYP2D6 and CYP3A4."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00662"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"388.1998"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00663"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"494.248"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Primarily hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00665"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"317.0623"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"The results of a human metabolism study using 14C-radiolabelled tablets show that nilutamide is extensively metabolized and less than 2% of the drug is excreted unchanged in urine after 5 days."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00665"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"317.0623"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"The results of a human metabolism study using 14C-radiolabelled tablets show that nilutamide is extensively metabolized and less than 2% of the drug is excreted unchanged in urine after 5 days."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00666"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"1321.6356"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Enzymatic hydrolysis."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00666"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"1321.6356"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Enzymatic hydrolysis."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00667"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"307.0334"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Primarily hepatic. Histamine is rapidly metabolized by methylation and oxidation. Methylation involves ring methylation and catalyzation by the enzyme histamine-N-methyltransferase, producing N-methylhistamine, which is mostly converted to N-methyl imidazole acetic acid. 2 to 3% excreted as free histamine, 4 to 8% as N-methylhistamine, 42 to 47% as N-methyl imidazole acetic acid, 9 to 11% as imidazole acetic acid, and 16 to 23% as imidazole acetic acid riboside"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00668"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"183.0895"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Epinephrine is rapidly inactivated in the body and is degraded by enzymes in the liver and other tissues. The larger portion of injected doses is excreted in the urine as inactivated compounds and the remainder either partly unchanged or conjugated. The drug becomes fixed in the tissues and is inactivated chiefly by enzymatic transformation to metanephrine or normetanephrine either of which is subsequently conjugated and excreted in the urine in the form of sulfates and glucuronides. Either sequence results in the formation of 3-methoxy-4-hydroxy-mandelic acid which also is detectable in the urine."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00668"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"183.0895"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Epinephrine is rapidly inactivated in the body and is degraded by enzymes in the liver and other tissues. The larger portion of injected doses is excreted in the urine as inactivated compounds and the remainder either partly unchanged or conjugated. The drug becomes fixed in the tissues and is inactivated chiefly by enzymatic transformation to metanephrine or normetanephrine either of which is subsequently conjugated and excreted in the urine in the form of sulfates and glucuronides. Either sequence results in the formation of 3-methoxy-4-hydroxy-mandelic acid which also is detectable in the urine."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00669"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"295.1354"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic. In vitro studies with human microsomes suggest that sumatriptan is metabolized by monoamine oxidase (MAO), predominantly the A isoenzyme."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00669"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"295.1354"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic. In vitro studies with human microsomes suggest that sumatriptan is metabolized by monoamine oxidase (MAO), predominantly the A isoenzyme."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00670"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"351.1695"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00670"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"351.1695"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00672"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"276.0335"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00673"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"534.1502"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Aprepitant is metabolized primarily by CYP3A4 with minor metabolism by CYP1A2 and CYP2C19. Seven metabolites of aprepitant, which are only weakly active, have been identified in human plasma."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00674"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"287.1521"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00674"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"287.1521"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00675"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"371.2249"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic. Tamoxifen is extensively metabolized after oral administration. N-desmethyl tamoxifen is the major metabolite found in plasma. N-desmethyl tamoxifen activity is similar to tamoxifen. 4-Hydroxytamoxifen and a side chain primary alcohol derivative of tamoxifen have been identified as minor metabolites in plasma. Tamoxifen is a substrate of cytochrome P450 CYP3A4, CYP2C9 and CYP2D6, and an inhibitor of P-glycoprotein."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00675"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"371.2249"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic. Tamoxifen is extensively metabolized after oral administration. N-desmethyl tamoxifen is the major metabolite found in plasma. N-desmethyl tamoxifen activity is similar to tamoxifen. 4-Hydroxytamoxifen and a side chain primary alcohol derivative of tamoxifen have been identified as minor metabolites in plasma. Tamoxifen is a substrate of cytochrome P450 CYP3A4, CYP2C9 and CYP2D6, and an inhibitor of P-glycoprotein."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00677"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"184.0665"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00677"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"184.0665"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00497"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"315.1471"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00497"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"315.1471"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00498"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"222.0681"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00498"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"222.0681"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00499"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"276.0722"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Flutamide is rapidly and extensively metabolized, with flutamide comprising only 2.5% of plasma radioactivity 1 hour after administration."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00499"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"276.0722"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Flutamide is rapidly and extensively metabolized, with flutamide comprising only 2.5% of plasma radioactivity 1 hour after administration."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00500"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"257.1052"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Essentially all of the administered dose is recovered in the urine in 24 hours either as an inactive oxidative metabolite or as conjugates of tolmetin."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00500"},"mass":{"type":"literal","v