ral","value":"277.1831"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic. Maprotiline is metabolized by N-demethylation, deamination, aliphatic and aromatic hydroxylations and by formation of aromatic methoxy derivatives."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00935"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"260.1889"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00935"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"260.1889"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00936"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"138.0317"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00936"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"138.0317"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00937"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"241.1233"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Extensively metabolized through a complex pathway of biotransformation involving N-dealkylation and reduction. Many of these metabolites are biologically active and may participate in the therapeutic action of diethylpropion."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00915"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"151.1361"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"No appreciable metabolism, although negligible amounts of an acetyl metabolite have been identified."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00915"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"151.1361"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"No appreciable metabolism, although negligible amounts of an acetyl metabolite have been identified."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00917"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"352.225"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Rapid metabolism of dinoprostone occurs primarily in the local tissues; any systemic absorption of the medication is cleared mainly in the maternal lungs and, secondarily, at sites such as the liver and kidneys."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00917"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"352.225"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Rapid metabolism of dinoprostone occurs primarily in the local tissues; any systemic absorption of the medication is cleared mainly in the maternal lungs and, secondarily, at sites such as the liver and kidneys."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00918"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"469.1883"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00920"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"425.1297"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Primarily hepatic. The main metabolite found in both plasma and urine is the inactive ketotifen-N-glucuronide. Nor-ketotifen, the N-demethylated metabolite, and the 10-alpha-hydroxyl derivative are the only other metabolites detectable in human urine."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00921"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"467.3036"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic. Buprenorphine undergoes both N-dealkylation to norbuprenorphine and glucuronidation. The N-dealkylation pathway is mediated by cytochrome P-450 3A4 isozyme. Norbuprenorphine, an active metabolite, can further undergo glucuronidation."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00921"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"467.3036"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic. Buprenorphine undergoes both N-dealkylation to norbuprenorphine and glucuronidation. The N-dealkylation pathway is mediated by cytochrome P-450 3A4 isozyme. Norbuprenorphine, an active metabolite, can further undergo glucuronidation."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00922"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"280.1073"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Complete metabolism, with some active metabolites (OR-1855 and OR-1896) possibly extending the drug's haemodynamic effects."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00924"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"275.1674"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Extensively metabolized (gastrointestinal and hepatic)."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00924"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"275.1674"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Extensively metabolized (gastrointestinal and hepatic)."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00925"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"303.139"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00925"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"303.139"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00926"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"354.2195"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Extensively metabolized, with significant first-pass metabolism to the pharmacologically active acid form. Subsequent metabolism results in the inactive 13-cis acid form, chain-shortened breakdown products, and conjugates that are ultimately excreted."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00927"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"337.0449"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00600"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"200.0837"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00600"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"200.0837"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00603"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"344.2351"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00603"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"344.2351"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00604"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"465.1831"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic. Extensively metabolized via cytochrome P450 3A4 enzyme."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00604"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"465.1831"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic. Extensively metabolized via cytochrome P450 3A4 enzyme."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00605"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"356.0882"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Undergoes two major biotransformations: reversible reduction to the sulfide metabolite, and irreversible oxidation to the sulfone metabolite. Available evidence indicates that the biological activity resides with the sulfide metabolite."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00605"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"356.0882"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Undergoes two major biotransformations: reversible reduction to the sulfide metabolite, and irreversible oxidation to the sulfone metabolite. Available evidence indicates that the biological activity resides with the sulfide metabolite."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00606"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"389.0271"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00606"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"389.0271"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00610"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"167.0946"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00610"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"167.0946"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00611"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"477.2363"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Extensively metabolized in the liver. The pharmacological activity of butorphanol metabolites has not been studied in humans; in animal studies, butorphanol metabolites have demonstrated some analgesic activity."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00612"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"325.2253"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00612"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"325.2253"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00617"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"157.0739"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Primarily hepatic (mainly via cytochrome P450 isozyme 2C9), paramethadione is completely demethylated to 5-ethyl-5-methyl-2,4-oxazolidinedione, the active metabolite."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00617"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"157.0739"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Primarily hepatic (mainly via cytochrome P450 isozyme 2C9), paramethadione is completely demethylated to 5-ethyl-5-methyl-2,4-oxazolidinedione, the active metabolite."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00620"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"394.1792"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00621"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"306.2195"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Renal"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00621"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"306.2195"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Renal"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00622"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"479.2056"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Nicardipine HCl is metabolized extensively by the liver."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00622"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"479.2056"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Nicardipine HCl is metabolized extensively by the liver."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00623"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"437.1749"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00623"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"437.1749"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01003"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"468.0693"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01003"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"468.0693"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01005"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"76.0273"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01005"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"76.0273"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01006"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"285.1014"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Primarily hepatic via CYP3A4 and CYP2A6. Letrozole inhibits the aromatase enzyme by competitively binding to the heme of the cytochrome P450 subunit of the enzyme, resulting in a reduction of estrogen biosynthesis in all tissues. It is metabolized slowly to an inactive metabolite whose glucuronide conjugate is excreted renally, representing the major clearance pathway."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01006"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"285.1014"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Primarily hepatic via CYP3A4 and CYP2A6. Letrozole inhibits the aromatase enzyme by competitively binding to the heme of the cytochrome P450 subunit of the enzyme, resulting in a reduction of estrogen biosynthesis in all tissues. It is metabolized slowly to an inactive metabolite whose glucuronide conjugate is excreted renally, representing the major clearance pathway."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01008"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"246.0232"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Mainly Hepatic. Busulfan is predominantly metabolized by conjugation with glutathione, both spontaneously and by glutathione S-transferase (GST) catalysis."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01009"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"254.0943"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01009"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"254.0943"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01010"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"201.092"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01011"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"226.1106"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic. The major biotransformation is reduction of the ketone to metyrapol, an active alcohol metabolite. Metyrapone and metyrapol are both conjugated with glucuronide."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01011"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"226.1106"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic. The major biotransformation is reduction of the ketone to metyrapol, an active alcohol metabolite. Metyrapone and metyrapol are both conjugated with glucuronide."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01012"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"357.1704"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Metabolism is hepatic by multiple enzymes, primarily CYP3A4, CYP2D6, and CYP1A2. After administration of a 75 mg radiolabeled dose to healthy volunteers, cinacalcet was rapidly and extensively metabolized via: 1) oxidative N-dealkylation to hydrocinnamic acid and hydroxy-hydrocinnamic acid, which are further metabolized via \u0026szlig;-oxidation and glycine conjugation; the oxidative N-dealkylation process also generates metabolites that contain the naphthalene ring; and 2) oxidation of the naphthalene ring on the parent drug to form dihydrodiols, which are further conjugated with glucuronic acid."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01013"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"466.1922"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Metabolized, primarily in the liver, and then excreted by the kidneys."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01014"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"357.0961"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Cleaved in the colon via bacterial azoreduction to 5\u0026ndash;aminosalicylic acid (5\u0026ndash;ASA) and 4\u0026ndash;aminobenzoyl-beta-alanine, the inactive carrier moiety."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01016"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"493.1438"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Primarily hepatic (mainly cytochrome P450 3A4). The major metabolite is the 4-trans-hydroxy derivative. A second metabolite, the 3-cis-hydroxy derivative, also occurs. These metabolites contribute no significant hypoglycemic action in humans as they are only weakly active."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01016"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"493.1438"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Primarily hepatic (mainly cytochrome P450 3A4). The major metabolite is the 4-trans-hydroxy derivative. A second metabolite, the 3-cis-hydroxy derivative, also occurs. These metabolites contribute no significant hypoglycemic action in humans as they are only weakly active."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01018"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"245.0123"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01018"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"245.0123"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01019"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"161.129"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01020"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"191.043"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01020"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"191.043"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01021"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"378.9022"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01021"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"378.9022"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00624"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"288.2089"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Testosterone is metabolized to 17-keto steroids through two different pathways. The major active metabolites are estradiol and dihydrotestosterone (DHT)."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00624"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"288.2089"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Testosterone is metabolized to 17-keto steroids through two different pathways. The major active metabolites are estradiol and dihydrotestosterone (DHT)."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00627"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"123.032"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00627"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"123.032"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00628"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"314.0458"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"The drug is metabolized in the liver and excreted primarily in the urine. The primary metabolite, nordiazepam, is further metabolized by hydroxylation. The major urinary metabolite is conjugated oxazepam (3-hydroxynordiazepam), and smaller amounts of conjugated p-hydroxynordiazepam and nordiazepam are also found in the urine."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00629"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"230.0126"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00629"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"230.0126"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00630"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"249.0167"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"There is no evidence that alendronate is metabolized in humans or animals."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00630"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"249.0167"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"There is no evidence that alendronate is metabolized in humans or animals."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00631"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"303.0534"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Clofarabine is sequentially metabolized intracellularly to the 5\u0026rsquo;-monophosphate metabolite by deoxycytidine kinase and mono- and di-phosphokinases to the active 5\u0026rsquo;-triphosphate metabolite. Clofarabine has high affinity for the activating phosphorylating enzyme, deoxycytidine kinase, equal to or greater than that of the natural substrate, deoxycytidine."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00633"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"200.1313"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00633"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"200.1313"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00635"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"358.178"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00636"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"242.071"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic and gastrointestinal: rapid de-esterification occurs in the gastrointestinal tract and/or on first-pass metabolism to produce the active form, clofibric acid (chlorophenoxy isobutyric acid [CPIB])."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00636"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"242.071"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic and gastrointestinal: rapid de-esterification occurs in the gastrointestinal tract and/or on first-pass metabolism to produce the active form, clofibric acid (chlorophenoxy isobutyric acid [CPIB])."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00637"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"458.2482"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Almost completely metabolized in the liver and primarily excreted in the feces."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00637"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"458.2482"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Almost completely metabolized in the liver and primarily excreted in the feces."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00640"},"m