o the total activity of the parent compound."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00561"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"378.2307"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00562"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"430.9835"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00562"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"430.9835"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00914"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"205.1327"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00914"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"205.1327"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00938"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"415.2723"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic, metabolized by hydroxylation via CYP3A4"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00938"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"415.2723"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic, metabolized by hydroxylation via CYP3A4"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00939"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"295.0167"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic. Meclofenamic acid is extensively metabolized to an active metabolite (Metabolite I; 3-hydroxymethyl metabolite of meclofenamic acid) and at least six other less well characterized minor metabolites. Only Metabolite I has been shown in vitro to inhibit cyclooxygenase activity with approximately one fifth the activity of meclofenamic acid."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00939"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"295.0167"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic. Meclofenamic acid is extensively metabolized to an active metabolite (Metabolite I; 3-hydroxymethyl metabolite of meclofenamic acid) and at least six other less well characterized minor metabolites. Only Metabolite I has been shown in vitro to inhibit cyclooxygenase activity with approximately one fifth the activity of meclofenamic acid."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00940"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"340.1913"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic, by enzymatic hydrolysis."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00941"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"660.1715"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00944"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"714.2355"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00945"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"180.0423"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Aspirin is rapidly hydrolyzed primarily in the liver to salicylic acid, which is conjugated with glycine (forming salicyluric acid) and glucuronic acid and excreted largely in the urine."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00945"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"180.0423"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Aspirin is rapidly hydrolyzed primarily in the liver to salicylic acid, which is conjugated with glycine (forming salicyluric acid) and glucuronic acid and excreted largely in the urine."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00946"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"282.1256"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Phenprocoumon is stereoselectively metabolized by hepatic microsomal enzymes (cytochrome P-450) to inactive hydroxylated metabolites (predominant route) and by reductases to reduced metabolites. Cytochrome P450 2C9 is the principal form of human liver P-450 responsible for metabolism."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00947"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"606.3166"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Metabolism of fulvestrant appears to involve combinations of a number of possible biotransformation pathways analogous to those of endogenous steroids, including oxidation, aromatic hydroxylation, conjugation with glucuronic acid and/or sulphate at the 2, 3 and 17 positions of the steroid nucleus, and oxidation of the side chain sulphoxide. Identified metabolites are either less active or exhibit similar activity to fulvestrant in antiestrogen models. Studies using human liver preparations and recombinant human enzymes indicate that cytochrome P-450 3A4 (CYP 3A4) is the only P-450 isoenzyme involved in the oxidation of fulvestrant; however, the relative contribution of P-450 and non-P-450 routes in vivo is unknown."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00949"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"238.0954"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00949"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"238.0954"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00950"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"501.2879"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Approximately 5% of the total dose is metabolized, by cytochrome P450 3A4 and by intestinal microflora."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00950"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"501.2879"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Approximately 5% of the total dose is metabolized, by cytochrome P450 3A4 and by intestinal microflora."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00928"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"244.0808"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"An in vitro study of azacitidine incubation in human liver fractions indicated that azacitidine may be metabolized by the liver. The potential of azacitidine to inhibit cytochrome P450 (CYP) enzymes is not known."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00928"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"244.0808"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"An in vitro study of azacitidine incubation in human liver fractions indicated that azacitidine may be metabolized by the liver. The potential of azacitidine to inhibit cytochrome P450 (CYP) enzymes is not known."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00929"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"382.2719"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Rapidly de-esterified to misoprostol acid. The de-esterified metabolite undergoes further metabolism by beta and omega oxidation; oxidation is followed by reduction of the ketone to yield prostaglandin F analogs."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00933"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"386.1487"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00933"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"386.1487"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00881"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"438.2155"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00881"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"438.2155"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00882"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"405.1859"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00882"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"405.1859"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00883"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"236.0281"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00883"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"236.0281"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00884"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"283.0011"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"No evidence found for metabolization of risedronate in humans or mammals"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00884"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"283.0011"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"No evidence found for metabolization of risedronate in humans or mammals"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00885"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"266.0318"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic (Ophthalmic)"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00887"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"364.1093"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"45% is secreted unchanged. Urinary and biliary metabolites are formed by oxidation of the N-butyl side chain."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00888"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"155.0269"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Undergoes rapid chemical transformation and combines with water or reactive compounds of cells, so that the drug is no longer present in active form a few minutes after administration."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00888"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"155.0269"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Undergoes rapid chemical transformation and combines with water or reactive compounds of cells, so that the drug is no longer present in active form a few minutes after administration."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00889"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"312.195"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Primarily hepatic; undergoes N -demethylation and aromatic ring oxidation followed by conjugation. Animal studies suggest that some of the metabolites may have 5-HT 3 receptor antagonist activity."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00889"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"312.195"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Primarily hepatic; undergoes N -demethylation and aromatic ring oxidation followed by conjugation. Animal studies suggest that some of the metabolites may have 5-HT 3 receptor antagonist activity."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00890"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"266.1307"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00890"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"266.1307"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00891"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"249.0572"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00894"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"300.1725"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic. Metabolized to several derivatives in the liver, all of which preserve the lactone D-ring."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00894"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"300.1725"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic. Metabolized to several derivatives in the liver, all of which preserve the lactone D-ring."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00896"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"370.2508"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Undergoes extensive metabolism. Following intravenous administration of radiolabeled rimexolone in rats, more than 80% of the dose was excreted in the feces as rimexolone and metabolites. Metabolites have been shown to be either less active than rimexolone or inactive in human glucocorticoid receptor binding assays."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00864"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"803.482"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic, extensive, primarily by CYP3A4. The major metabolite identified in incubations with human liver microsomes is 13-demethyl tacrolimus. In in vitro studies, a 31-demethyl metabolite has been reported to have the same activity as tacrolimus."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00864"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"803.482"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic, extensive, primarily by CYP3A4. The major metabolite identified in incubations with human liver microsomes is 13-demethyl tacrolimus. In in vitro studies, a 31-demethyl metabolite has been reported to have the same activity as tacrolimus."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00865"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"239.1674"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic. Benzphetamine's metabolites include amphetamine and methamphetamine."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00865"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"239.1674"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic. Benzphetamine's metabolites include amphetamine and methamphetamine."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00866"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"249.1729"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic. One of the active metabolites, 4-OH-alprenolol, is an active beta-blocker."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00867"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"287.1521"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic, by both the mother and fetus"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00867"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"287.1521"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic, by both the mother and fetus"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00868"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"603.3619"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Benzonatate is hydrolyzed to para-aminobenzoic acid (PABA) by plasma esterases"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00869"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"324.0272"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00869"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"324.0272"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00870"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"260.0507"},"transform":