e":"literal","value":"424.1457"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Eprosartan is not metabolized by the cytochrome P450 system. It is mainly eliminated as unchanged drug. Less than 2% of an oral dose is excreted in the urine as a glucuronide."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00877"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"913.5551"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00877"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"913.5551"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00880"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"294.9488"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Chlorothiazide is not metabolized but is eliminated rapidly by the kidney."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00880"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"294.9488"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Chlorothiazide is not metabolized but is eliminated rapidly by the kidney."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01122"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"536.2685"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Plasma and hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01124"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"270.1038"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01124"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"270.1038"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01125"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"252.0786"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01126"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"528.2211"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic. Extensively metabolized."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01128"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"430.061"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Bicalutamide undergoes stereo specific metabolism. The S (inactive) isomer is metabolized primarily by glucuronidation. The R (active) isomer also undergoes glucuronidation but is predominantly oxidized to an inactive metabolite followed by glucuronidation."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01128"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"430.061"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Bicalutamide undergoes stereo specific metabolism. The S (inactive) isomer is metabolized primarily by glucuronidation. The R (active) isomer also undergoes glucuronidation but is predominantly oxidized to an inactive metabolite followed by glucuronidation."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01129"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"359.1304"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01129"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"359.1304"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01130"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"488.241"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Primarily in skin"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01132"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"356.1195"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01132"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"356.1195"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01133"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"317.9284"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"In vitro, tiludronic acid is not metabolized in human liver microsomes and hepatocytes. There is no evidence that tiludronate is metabolized in humans."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01133"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"317.9284"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"In vitro, tiludronic acid is not metabolized in human liver microsomes and hepatocytes. There is no evidence that tiludronate is metabolized in humans."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01134"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"414.277"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01135"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"1104.4728"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"In vivo data from humans suggest that doxacurium chloride is not metabolized and that the major elimination pathway is excretion of unchanged drug in urine and bile."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01136"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"406.1893"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic. Carvedilol is metabolized primarily by aromatic ring oxidation and glucuronidation. The oxidative metabolites are further metabolized by conjugation via glucuronidation and sulfation. Demethylation and hydroxylation at the phenol ring produce three active metabolites with b-receptor blocking activity. The 4'-hydroxyphenyl metabolite is approximately 13 times more potent than carvedilol for b-blockade."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01136"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"406.1893"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic. Carvedilol is metabolized primarily by aromatic ring oxidation and glucuronidation. The oxidative metabolites are further metabolized by conjugation via glucuronidation and sulfation. Demethylation and hydroxylation at the phenol ring produce three active metabolites with b-receptor blocking activity. The 4'-hydroxyphenyl metabolite is approximately 13 times more potent than carvedilol for b-blockade."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01138"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"404.1195"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01138"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"404.1195"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01142"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"279.1623"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01142"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"279.1623"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01143"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"214.0541"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Amifostine is rapidly dephosphorylated by alkaline phosphatase in tissues primarily to the active free thiol metabolite and, subsequently, to a less active disulfide metabolite."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00969"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"294.1481"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic, via microsomal cytochrome P450 (CYP)"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00970"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"1254.6285"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00970"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"1254.6285"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00972"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"381.1608"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Azelastine hydrochloride is oxidatively metabolized to the principal metabolite, N-desmethylazelastine, by the cytochrome P450 enzyme system, however the exact cytochrome P450 isoenzyme involved has not been determined. The major metabolite, desmethylazelastine, also has H1-receptor antagonist activity."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00972"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"381.1608"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Azelastine hydrochloride is oxidatively metabolized to the principal metabolite, N-desmethylazelastine, by the cytochrome P450 enzyme system, however the exact cytochrome P450 isoenzyme involved has not been determined. The major metabolite, desmethylazelastine, also has H1-receptor antagonist activity."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00973"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"409.149"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic, intestinal wall"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00974"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"374.0015"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Almost none of the compound is metabolized."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00975"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"504.3173"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00977"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"296.1776"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic. Quantitatively, the major metabolic pathway for ethinyl estradiol, both in rats and in humans, is aromatic hydroxylation, as it is for the natural estrogens."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00977"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"296.1776"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic. Quantitatively, the major metabolic pathway for ethinyl estradiol, both in rats and in humans, is aromatic hydroxylation, as it is for the natural estrogens."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00979"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"291.1834"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00979"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"291.1834"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00980"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"259.1572"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00981"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"275.1634"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Quickly hydrolyzed by cholinesterases"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00981"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"275.1634"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Quickly hydrolyzed by cholinesterases"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00982"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"300.2089"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00983"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"344.1736"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Metabolized primarily by direct glucuronidation at either the phenolic or aliphatic hydroxyl group and O-demethylation followed by glucuronide conjugation at either phenolic hydroxyl groups. Minor pathways involve sulfate conjugation of formoterol and deformylation followed by sulfate conjugation. The most prominent pathway involves direct conjugation at the phenolic hydroxyl group. The second major pathway involves O-demethylation followed by conjugation at the phenolic 2'-hydroxyl group. Four cytochrome P450 isozymes (CYP2D6, CYP2C19, CYP2C9 and CYP2A6) are involved in the O-demethylation of formoterol."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00983"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"344.1736"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Metabolized primarily by direct glucuronidation at either the phenolic or aliphatic hydroxyl group and O-demethylation followed by glucuronide conjugation at either phenolic hydroxyl groups. Minor pathways involve sulfate conjugation of formoterol and deformylation followed by sulfate conjugation. The most prominent pathway involves direct conjugation at the phenolic hydroxyl group. The second major pathway involves O-demethylation followed by conjugation at the phenolic 2'-hydroxyl group. Four cytochrome P450 isozymes (CYP2D6, CYP2C19, CYP2C9 and CYP2A6) are involved in the O-demethylation of formoterol."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00984"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"274.1933"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Nandrolone is unusual in that unlike most anabolic steroids, it is not broken down into the more reactive DHT by the enzyme 5??-reductase, but rather into a less effective product known as Dihydronandrolone."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00984"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"274.1933"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Nandrolone is unusual in that unlike most anabolic steroids, it is not broken down into the more reactive DHT by the enzyme 5??-reductase, but rather into a less effective product known as Dihydronandrolone."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00985"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"469.1881"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic (cytochrome P-450 system)."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01168"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"221.1528"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Procarbazine is metabolized primarily in the liver and kidneys. The drug appears to be auto-oxidized to the azo derivative with the release of hydrogen peroxide. The azo derivative isomerizes to the hydrazone, and following hydrolysis splits into a benzylaldehyde derivative and methylhydrazine. The methylhydrazine is further degraded to CO\u003csub\u003e2\u003c/sub\u003e and CH\u003csub\u003e4\u003c/sub\u003e and possibly hydrazine, whereas the aldehyde is oxidized to N-isopropylterephthalamic acid, which is excreted in the urine."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01168"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"221.1528"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Procarbazine is metabolized primarily in the liver and kidneys. The drug appears to be auto-oxidized to the azo derivative with the release of hydrogen peroxide. The azo derivative isomerizes to the hydrazone, and following hydrolysis splits into a benzylaldehyde derivative and methylhydrazine. The methylhydrazine is further degraded to CO\u003csub\u003e2\u003c/sub\u003e and CH\u003csub\u003e4\u003c/sub\u003e and possibly hydrazine, whereas the aldehyde is oxidized to N-isopropylterephthalamic acid, which is excreted in the urine."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01169"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"197.8279"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"The metabolism of arsenic trioxide involves reduction of pentavalent arsenic to trivalent arsenic by arsenate reductase and methylation of trivalent arsenic to monomethylarsonic acid and monomethylarsonic acid to dimethylarsinic acid by methyltransferases. The main site of methylation reactions appears to be the liver. Arsenic is stored mainly in liver, kidney, heart, lung, hair and nails."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01170"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"296.1518"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Guanethidine is converted by the liver to three metabolites, which are excreted in the urine. The metabolites are pharmacologically less active than the parent compound."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01171"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"268.0979"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Moclobemide is almost completely metabolized in the liver by Cytochrome P450 2C19 and 2D6."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01173"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"269.178"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Biotransformation occurs mainly in the liver. Pharmacologically active metabolites are N-demethyl orphenadrine and N,N-didemethyl orphenadrine."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01174"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"232.0848"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic (mostly via CYP2C19)."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01174"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"232.0848"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic (mostly via CYP2C19)."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01175"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"324.1638"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Mainly hepatic. Escitalopram undergoes N-demethylation to S-demethylcitalopram (S-DCT) and S-didemethylcitalopram (S-DDCT). CYP3A4 and CYP2C19 are the enzymes responsible for this N-demethylation."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01176"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"266.1783"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Cyclizine is metabolised to its N-demethylated derivative, norcyclizine, which has little antihistaminic (H1) activity compared to Cyclizine."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01176"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"266.1783"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Cyclizine is metabolised to its N-demethylated derivative, norcyclizine, which has little antihistaminic (H1) activity compared to Cyclizine."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01178"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"273.0226"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01180"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"634.289"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01180"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"634.289"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01181"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"260.0248"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Primarily hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01181"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"260.0248"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Primarily hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01182"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"341.1991"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Metabolized primarily in the liver where it is rapidly and extensively metabolized to two active metabolites, 5-hydroxypropafenone and N-depropylpropafenone. These metabolites have antiarrhythmic activity comparable to propafenone but are present in concentrations less than 25% of propafenone concentrations."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01182"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"341.1991"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Metabolized primarily in the liver where it is rapidly and extensively metabolized to two active metabolites, 5-hydroxypropafenone and N-depropylpropafenone. These metabolites have antiarrhythmic activity comparable to propafenone but are present in concentrations less than 25% of propafenone concentrations."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01183"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"327.1471"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01183"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"327.1471"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01184"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"425.1619"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00897"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"342.0439"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic. Small amounts of unmetabolized triazolam appear in the urine."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00898"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"46.0419"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic. Metabolized by cytochrome P450 enzyme CYP2E1."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00898"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"46.0419"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic. Metabolized by cytochrome P450 enzyme CYP2E1."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00899"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"376.1998"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"By hydrolysis of the propanoic acid-methyl ester linkage by nonspecific blood and tissue esterases."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00899"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"376.1998"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"By hydrolysis of the propanoic acid-methyl ester linkage by nonspecific blood and tissue esterases."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00901"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"461.2202"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00901"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"461.2202"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00902"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"296.1347"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00902"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"296.1347"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00903"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"302.0113"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00904"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"293.1528"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00904"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"293.1528"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00905"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"415.2723"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Bimatoprost undergoes oxidation, N-deethylation and glucuronidation to form a variety of metabolites."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00906"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"375.1327"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Tiagabine is likely metabolized primarily by the 3A isoform subfamily of hepatic cytochrome P450."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00906"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"375.1327"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Tiagabine is likely metabolized primarily by the 3A isoform subfamily of hepatic cytochrome P450."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00907"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"303.1471"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic. Cocaine is metabolized to benzoylecgonine and ecgonine methyl ester, which are both excreted in the urine. In the presence of alcohol, a further active metabolite, cocaethylene is formed, and is more toxic then cocaine itself."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00907"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"303.1471"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic. Cocaine is metabolized to benzoylecgonine and ecgonine methyl ester, which are both excreted in the urine. In the presence of alcohol, a further active metabolite, cocaethylene is formed, and is more toxic then cocaine itself."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00908"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"324.1838"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00908"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"324.1838"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00909"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"212.0256"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Primarily hepatic through cytochrome P450 isoenzyme 3A4 (CYP3A4). Undergoes acetylation and reduction, forming N-acetyl zonisamide, and the open-ring metabolite 2\u0026ndash;sulfamoylacetyl phenol, respectively."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00909"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"212.0256"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Primarily hepatic through cytochrome P450 isoenzyme 3A4 (CYP3A4). Undergoes acetylation and reduction, forming N-acetyl zonisamide, and the open-ring metabolite 2\u0026ndash;sulfamoylacetyl phenol, respectively."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00910"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"416.329"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Metabolized by multiple hepatic and non-hepatic enzymes, including mitochondrial CYP24, as well as CYP3A4 and UGT1A4"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00910"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"416.329"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Metabolized by multiple hepatic and non-hepatic enzymes, including mitochondrial CYP24, as well as CYP3A4 and UGT1A4"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00912"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"452.2675"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Repaglinide is completely metabolized by oxidative biotransformation and direct conjugation with glucuronic acid after either an IV or oral dose."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00912"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"452.2675"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Repaglinide is completely metabolized by oxidative biotransformation and direct conjugation with glucuronic acid after either an IV or oral dose."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00913"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"352.2151"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01104"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"305.0738"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01104"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"305.0738"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01105"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"279.1754"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01105"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"279.1754"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01107"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"183.1259"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic. Methyprylon is almost completely metabolized."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01108"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"329.1991"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01113"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"375.1237"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01114"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"274.1237"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Primarily hepatic via Cytochrome P450 (CYP450) enzymes."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01114"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"274.1237"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Primarily hepatic via Cytochrome P450 (CYP450) enzymes."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01115"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"346.1165"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01115"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"346.1165"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01116"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"365.1688"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01118"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"645.0237"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Amiodarone is extensively metabolized in the liver via CYP2C8 (under 1% unchanged in urine), and can effect the metabolism of numerous other drugs. The major metabolite of amiodarone is desethylamiodarone (DEA), which also has antiarrhythmic properties. The metabolism of amiodarone is inhibited by grapefruit juice, leading to elevated serum levels of amiodarone."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01118"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"645.0237"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Amiodarone is extensively metabolized in the liver via CYP2C8 (under 1% unchanged in urine), and can effect the metabolism of numerous other drugs. The major metabolite of amiodarone is desethylamiodarone (DEA), which also has antiarrhythmic properties. The metabolism of amiodarone is inhibited by grapefruit juice, leading to elevated serum levels of amiodarone."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01119"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"229.9917"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01119"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"229.9917"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01120"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"323.1304"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01121"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"178.0742"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Metabolized in the liver by hepatic microsomal enzymes, where it is inactivated by p-hydroxylation."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01121"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"178.0742"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Metabolized in the liver by hepatic microsomal enzymes, where it is inactivated by p-hydroxylation."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00812"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"308.1525"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00812"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"308.1525"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00813"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"336.2202"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Fentanyl is metabolized primarily via human cytochrome P450 3A4 isoenzyme system."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00814"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"351.0347"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00814"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"351.0347"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00815"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"288.1371"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00815"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"288.1371"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00816"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"211.1208"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic and gastric. The major metabolite, orciprenaline-3-0-sulfate, is produced in the gastrointestinal tract. Orciprenaline is not metabolized by catechol-0-methyltransferase nor have glucuronide conjugates been isolated to date."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00816"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"211.1208"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic and gastric. The major metabolite, orciprenaline-3-0-sulfate, is produced in the gastrointestinal tract. Orciprenaline is not metabolized by catechol-0-methyltransferase nor have glucuronide conjugates been isolated to date."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00818"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"178.1358"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00818"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"178.1358"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00997"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"543.1741"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00997"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"543.1741"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00998"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"243.1372"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"In vitro, cytochrome P450 1A2 appears to be the principal enzyme involved in the metabolism of frovatriptan to several metabolites including hydroxylated frovatriptan, N-acetyl desmethyl frovatriptan, hydroxylated N-acetyl desmethyl frovatriptan and desmethyl frovatriptan, and several other minor metabolites. Desmethyl frovatriptan has lower affinity for 5-HT\u003csub\u003e1B/1D\u003c/sub\u003e receptors compared to the parent compound. The N-acetyl desmethyl metabolite has no significant affinity for 5-HT receptors. The activity of the other metabolites is unknown."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00999"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"296.9645"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hydrochlorothiazide is not metabolized."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01001"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"239.1521"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hydrolyzed by esterases in tissue and blood to the active compound colterol. The drug is also conjugatively metabolized to salbutamol 4'-O-sulfate."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01002"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"288.2202"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Levobupivacaine is extensively metabolized with no unchanged levobupivacaine detected in urine or feces. In vitro studies using [14 C] levobupivacaine showed that CYP3A4 isoform and CYP1A2 isoform mediate the metabolism of levobupivacaine to desbutyl levobupivacaine and 3-hydroxy levobupivacaine, respectively. In vivo, the 3-hydroxy levobupivacaine appears to undergo further transformation to glucuronide and sulfate conjugates. Metabolic inversion of levobupivacaine to R(+)-bupivacaine was not evident both in vitro and in vivo."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01002"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"288.2202"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Levobupivacaine is extensively metabolized with no unchanged levobupivacaine detected in urine or feces. In vitro studies using [14 C] levobupivacaine showed that CYP3A4 isoform and CYP1A2 isoform mediate the metabolism of levobupivacaine to desbutyl levobupivacaine and 3-hydroxy levobupivacaine, respectively. In vivo, the 3-hydroxy levobupivacaine appears to undergo further transformation to glucuronide and sulfate conjugates. Metabolic inversion of levobupivacaine to R(+)-bupivacaine was not evident both in vitro and in vivo."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01254"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"487.1557"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Dasatinib is extensively metabolized in humans, primarily by the cytochrome P450 enzyme 3A4"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01258"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"551.3934"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic. About one-fourth of the absorbed dose appears in the urine as parent drug. How much of the absorbed dose is metabolized is unknown. Based on the in vitro studies, the major enzyme responsible for aliskiren metabolism appears to be CYP 3A4."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01259"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"942.1841"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Lapatinib undergoes extensive metabolism, primarily by CYP3A4 and CYP3A5, with minor contributions from CYP2C19 and CYP2C8 to a variety of oxidated metabolites, none of which accounts for more than 14% of the dose recovered in the feces or 10% of lapatinib concentration in plasma."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01260"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"416.2199"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01262"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"228.0859"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"The exact route of elimination and metabolic fate of decitabine is not known in humans.  One of the pathways of elimination of decitabine appears to be deamination by cytidine deaminase found principally in the liver but also in granulocytes, intestinal epithelium and whole blood."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01267"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"426.2067"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Although in vitro studies suggested a role for CYP2D6 and CYP3A4 in the metabolism of paliperidone, in vivo results indicate that these isozymes play a limited role in the overall elimination of paliperidone. Four primary metabolic pathways have been identified in vivo, none of which could be shown to account for more than 10% of the dose: dealkylation, hydroxylation, dehydrogenation, and benzisoxazole scission."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01270"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"48349.6111"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01272"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"105270.8023"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01039"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"360.1128"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01039"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"360.1128"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01041"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"258.0641"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Thalidomide itself does not appear to be hepatically metabolized to any large extent, but appears to undergo non-enzymatic hydrolysis in plasma to multiple metabolites. Thalidomide may be metabolized hepatically by enzymes of the cytochrome P450 enzyme system. The end product of metabolism, phthalic acid, is excreted as a glycine conjugate."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01041"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"258.0641"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Thalidomide itself does not appear to be hepatically metabolized to any large extent, but appears to undergo non-enzymatic hydrolysis in plasma to multiple metabolites. Thalidomide may be metabolized hepatically by enzymes of the cytochrome P450 enzyme system. The end product of metabolism, phthalic acid, is excreted as a glycine conjugate."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01042"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"304.0745"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Melphalan is not actively metabolised, it spontaneously degrades to mono and dihydroxy products."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01043"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"179.1674"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Excreted largely unchanged. About 20% is metabolized to 1-amino-3-hydroxymethyl-5-methyl-adamantane and 3-amino-1-hydroxy-5,7-dimethyl-adamantane."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01043"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"179.1674"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Excreted largely unchanged. About 20% is metabolized to 1-amino-3-hydroxymethyl-5-methyl-adamantane and 3-amino-1-hydroxy-5,7-dimethyl-adamantane."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01046"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"390.2218"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"The results of both human and animal studies indicate that lubiprostone is rapidly and extensively metabolized by 15-position reduction, \u0026alpha;-chain \u0026beta;-oxidation, and \u0026omega;-chain \u0026omega;-oxidation. These biotransformations are not mediated by the hepatic cytochrome P450 system but rather appear to be mediated by the ubiquitously expressed carbonyl reductase. M3, a metabolite of lubiprostone in both humans and animals is formed by the reduction of the carbonyl group at the 15-hydroxy moiety that consists of both \u0026alpha;-hydroxy and \u0026beta;-hydroxy epimers. M3 makes up less than 10% of the dose of radiolabeled lubiprostone."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01046"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"390.2218"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"The results of both human and animal studies indicate that lubiprostone is rapidly and extensively metabolized by 15-position reduction, \u0026alpha;-chain \u0026beta;-oxidation, and \u0026omega;-chain \u0026omega;-oxidation. These biotransformations are not mediated by the hepatic cytochrome P450 system but rather appear to be mediated by the ubiquitously expressed carbonyl reductase. M3, a metabolite of lubiprostone in both humans and animals is formed by the reduction of the carbonyl group at the 15-hydroxy moiety that consists of both \u0026alpha;-hydroxy and \u0026beta;-hydroxy epimers. M3 makes up less than 10% of the dose of radiolabeled lubiprostone."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01047"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"494.2116"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Primarily hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01050"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"206.1307"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01050"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"206.1307"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01054"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"360.1321"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01054"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"360.1321"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01055"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"198.0641"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01056"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"192.1263"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Negligible first pass hepatic degradation. No active metabolites have been found."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01056"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"192.1263"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Negligible first pass hepatic degradation. No active metabolites have been found."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01057"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"256.1136"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01062"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"357.2304"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic, primarily by CYP3A4"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01062"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"357.2304"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic, primarily by CYP3A4"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01063"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"411.198"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01064"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"211.1208"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01064"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"211.1208"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01022"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"450.3498"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01022"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"450.3498"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01023"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"383.0691"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01024"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"320.126"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Mycophenolic acid is metabolized mainly by glucuronyl transferase to glucuronidated metabolites, predominantly the phenolic glucuronide, mycophenolic acid glucuronide (MPAG). MPAG does not manifest pharmacological activity. The acyl glucuronide minor metabolite has pharmacological activity similar to mycophenolic acid. The AUC ratio of Mycophenolic acid:MPAG:acyl glucuronide is approximately 1:24:0.28 at steady state."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01025"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"298.0954"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Metabolized to hydroxylated and conjugated metabolites."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01028"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"163.9607"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01028"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"163.9607"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01029"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"428.2325"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic. Irbesartan is metabolized via glucuronide conjugation and oxidation. In vitro studies of irbesartan oxidation by cytochrome P450 isoenzymes indicated irbesartan was oxidized primarily by 2C9; metabolism by 3A4 was negligible."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01029"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"428.2325"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic. Irbesartan is metabolized via glucuronide conjugation and oxidation. In vitro studies of irbesartan oxidation by cytochrome P450 isoenzymes indicated irbesartan was oxidized primarily by 2C9; metabolism by 3A4 was negligible."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01030"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"457.1404"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Topotecan undergoes a reversible pH dependent hydrolysis of its lactone moiety; it is the lactone form that is pharmacologically active."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01031"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"167.0946"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01031"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"167.0946"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01032"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"285.1035"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01032"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"285.1035"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01033"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"152.0157"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic. Degradation primarily by xanthine oxidase. The catabolism of mercaptopurine and its metabolites is complex. In humans, after oral administration of \u003csup\u003e35\u003c/sup\u003eS-6-mercaptopurine, urine contains intact mercaptopurine, thiouric acid (formed by direct oxidation by xanthine oxidase, probably via 6-mercapto-8-hydroxypurine), and a number of 6-methylated thiopurines. The methylthiopurines yield appreciable amounts of inorganic sulfate."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01033"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"152.0157"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic. Degradation primarily by xanthine oxidase. The catabolism of mercaptopurine and its metabolites is complex. In humans, after oral administration of \u003csup\u003e35\u003c/sup\u003eS-6-mercaptopurine, urine contains intact mercaptopurine, thiouric acid (formed by direct oxidation by xanthine oxidase, probably via 6-mercapto-8-hydroxypurine), and a number of 6-methylated thiopurines. The methylthiopurines yield appreciable amounts of inorganic sulfate."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01034"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"223.1208"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01035"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"235.1685"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01035"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"235.1685"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01036"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"325.2406"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01036"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"325.2406"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01037"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"187.1361"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01037"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"187.1361"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01238"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"447.148"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01238"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"447.148"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01239"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"315.0848"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01239"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"315.0848"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01240"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"352.225"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Epoprostenol is metabolized to 2 primary metabolites: 6-keto-PGF1\u0026alpha; (formed by spontaneous degradation) and 6,15-diketo-13,14-dihydro-PGF1\u0026alpha; (enzymatically formed), both of which have pharmacological activity orders of magnitude less than epoprostenol in animal test systems. Fourteen additional minor metabolites have been isolated from urine, indicating that epoprostenol is extensively metabolized in humans."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01240"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"352.225"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Epoprostenol is metabolized to 2 primary metabolites: 6-keto-PGF1\u0026alpha; (formed by spontaneous degradation) and 6,15-diketo-13,14-dihydro-PGF1\u0026alpha; (enzymatically formed), both of which have pharmacological activity orders of magnitude less than epoprostenol in animal test systems. Fourteen additional minor metabolites have been isolated from urine, indicating that epoprostenol is extensively metabolized in humans."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01241"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"250.1569"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic. Gemfibrozil mainly undergoes oxidation of a ring methyl group to successively form a hydroxymethyl and a carboxyl metabolite."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01242"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"314.155"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic. The main active metabolite is desmethylclomipramine."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01242"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"314.155"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic. The main active metabolite is desmethylclomipramine."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01243"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"212.9748"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01244"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"366.2671"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01244"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"366.2671"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01245"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"258.3035"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01196"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"439.1681"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01196"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"439.1681"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01197"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"217.0773"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01198"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"388.1051"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Extensively metabolized in the liver via decarboxylation (major pathway), demethylation, and side chain oxidation. Metabolites include an N-oxide derivative (weakly active; approximately 12% of a dose) and an N-desmethyl metabolite (inactive; approximately 16%). Approximately 50% of a dose is converted to other inactive metabolites via decarboxylation. Hepatic microsomal enzymes are apparently not involved in zopiclone clearance."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01199"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"770.2948"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01200"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"653.2213"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01200"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"653.2213"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01065"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"232.1212"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic. Metabolized in the liver by hydroxylation to 6-hydroxymelatonin."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01065"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"232.1212"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic. Metabolized in the liver by hydroxylation to 6-hydroxymelatonin."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01067"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"445.1784"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic. The major metabolites of glipizide are products of aromatic hydroxylation and have no hypoglycemic activity. A minor metabolite which accounts for less than 2% of a dose, an acetylaminoethyl benzine derivatives, is reported to have 1/10 to 1/3 as much hypoglycemic activity as the parent compound."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01068"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"315.0411"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic (cytochrome P450, including CYP3A). Biotransformation occurs mainly by reduction of the 7-nitro group to the 4-amino derivative. This derivative can be acetylated, hydroxylated, and glucuronidated."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01069"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"284.1347"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01069"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"284.1347"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01070"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"398.3549"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01070"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"398.3549"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01071"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"322.1504"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01071"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"322.1504"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01073"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"365.0537"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01075"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"255.1623"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic and renal"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01076"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"558.253"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Atorvastatin is extensively metabolized to ortho- and parahydroxylated derivatives and various beta-oxidation products. In vitro inhibition of HMG-CoA reductase by ortho- and parahydroxylated metabolites is equivalent to that of atorvastatin. Approximately 70% of circulating inhibitory activity for HMG-CoA reductase is attributed to active metabolites."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01076"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"558.253"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Atorvastatin is extensively metabolized to ortho- and parahydroxylated derivatives and various beta-oxidation products. In vitro inhibition of HMG-CoA reductase by ortho- and parahydroxylated metabolites is equivalent to that of atorvastatin. Approximately 70% of circulating inhibitory activity for HMG-CoA reductase is attributed to active metabolites."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01077"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"249.9384"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Not metabolized."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01078"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"942.4824"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01079"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"417.2012"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Tegaserod is metabolized mainly via two pathways. The first is a presystemic acid catalyzed hydrolysis in the stomach followed by oxidation and conjugation which produces the main metabolite of tegaserod, 5-methoxyindole-3-carboxylic acid glucuronide. The main metabolite has negligible affinity for 5-HT\u003csub\u003e4\u003c/sub\u003e receptors in vitro. The second metabolic pathway of tegaserod is direct glucuronidation which leads to generation of three isomeric N-glucuronides."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01080"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"129.079"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Almost no metabolic transformation. Does not induce the hepatic cytochrome P450 system."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01080"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"129.079"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Almost no metabolic transformation. Does not induce the hepatic cytochrome P450 system."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01081"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"452.2464"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01081"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"452.2464"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00700"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"414.2042"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Eplerenone is metabolized primarily by CYP3A4, however, no active metabolites have been identified in human plasma."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00700"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"414.2042"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Eplerenone is metabolized primarily by CYP3A4, however, no active metabolites have been identified in human plasma."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00703"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"236.0038"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00703"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"236.0038"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00704"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"341.1627"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic. When administered orally, naltrexone undergoes extensive biotransformation and is metabolized to 6 beta-naltrexol (which may contribute to the therapeutic effect) and other minor metabolites."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00704"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"341.1627"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic. When administered orally, naltrexone undergoes extensive biotransformation and is metabolized to 6 beta-naltrexol (which may contribute to the therapeutic effect) and other minor metabolites."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00706"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"408.1719"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Tamsulosin HCI is extensively metabolized by cytochrome P450 enzymes in the liver, however, the pharmacokinetic profile of the metabolites in humans has not been established."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00706"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"408.1719"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Tamsulosin HCI is extensively metabolized by cytochrome P450 enzymes in the liver, however, the pharmacokinetic profile of the metabolites in humans has not been established."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00708"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"386.2028"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00708"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"386.2028"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00711"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"199.1685"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Partially metabolized to diethylcarbamazine N-oxide."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00711"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"199.1685"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Partially metabolized to diethylcarbamazine N-oxide."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00712"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"244.09"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic. Cytochrome P450 2C9 plays an important role in the metabolism of flurbiprofen to its major metabolite, 4\u0026rsquo;-hydroxy-flurbiprofen. The 4\u0026rsquo;-hydroxy-flurbiprofen metabolite showed little anti-inflammatory activity in animal models of inflammation."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00714"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"624.2158"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00715"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"329.1427"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00715"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"329.1427"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00716"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"371.1005"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Nedocromil is not metabolized after IV administration and is excreted unchanged."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00716"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"371.1005"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Nedocromil is not metabolized after IV administration and is excreted unchanged."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00717"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"298.1933"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00717"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"298.1933"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00719"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"290.1783"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00719"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"290.1783"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01083"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"495.3924"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Metabolized primarily within the gastrointestinal wall forming relatively inactive metabolites. Metabolites M1 (4-member lactone ring hydrolyzed) and M3 (M1 with N-formyl leucine moiety cleaved) accounted for approximately 42% of total radioactivity in plasma. M1 and M3 have an open beta-lactone ring and extremely weak lipase inhibitory activity (1000- and 2500-fold less than orlistat, respectively)."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01084"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"302.2107"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Two primary metabolites, 5-hydroxyemedastine and 6-hydroxyemedastine, are excreted in the urine as both free and conjugated forms. The 5'-oxoanalogs of 5-hydroxyemedastine and 6-hydroxy-emedastine and the N-oxide are also formed as minor metabolites."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01084"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"302.2107"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Two primary metabolites, 5-hydroxyemedastine and 6-hydroxyemedastine, are excreted in the urine as both free and conjugated forms. The 5'-oxoanalogs of 5-hydroxyemedastine and 6-hydroxy-emedastine and the N-oxide are also formed as minor metabolites."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01085"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"244.0979"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Possibly occurs at the neuronal synapses and in the plasma"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01086"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"165.079"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01086"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"165.079"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01088"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"360.2301"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Primarily hepatic. Iloprost is metabolized principally via beta-oxidation of the carboxyl side chain."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01089"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"578.2628"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01092"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"584.2833"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01093"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"78.0139"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Dimethyl sulfoxide is metabolized in man by oxidation to dimethyl sulfone or by reduction in dimethyl sulfide. Dimethyl sulfoxide and dimethyl sulfone are excreted in the urine and feces."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01093"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"78.0139"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Dimethyl sulfoxide is metabolized in man by oxidation to dimethyl sulfone or by reduction in dimethyl sulfide. Dimethyl sulfoxide and dimethyl sulfone are excreted in the urine and feces."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01094"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"302.079"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01095"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"411.1846"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01095"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"411.1846"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01097"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"270.0616"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Primarily hepatic. Leflunomide is converted to its active form following oral intake."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01097"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"270.0616"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Primarily hepatic. Leflunomide is converted to its active form following oral intake."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01100"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"461.2279"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Notable first-pass metabolism in the liver, primarily by N-dealkylation via the cytochrome P450 isoenzymes CYP3A and CYP1A2 (and possibly CYP2D6). The activity of the two major metabolites has not been determined."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01100"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"461.2279"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Notable first-pass metabolism in the liver, primarily by N-dealkylation via the cytochrome P450 isoenzymes CYP3A and CYP1A2 (and possibly CYP2D6). The activity of the two major metabolites has not been determined."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01101"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"359.1493"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Metabolized by thymidine phosphorylase to fluoruracil."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01101"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"359.1493"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Metabolized by thymidine phosphorylase to fluoruracil."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00952"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"335.1667"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Primarily hepatic. \u003ci\u003eIn vitro,\u003c/i\u003e naratriptan is metabolized by a wide range of cytochrome P450 isoenzymes into a number of inactive metabolites."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00952"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"335.1667"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Primarily hepatic. \u003ci\u003eIn vitro,\u003c/i\u003e naratriptan is metabolized by a wide range of cytochrome P450 isoenzymes into a number of inactive metabolites."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00953"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"391.2008"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Rizatriptan is metabolized by monoamine oxidase A isoenzyme (MAO-A) to an inactive indole acetic acid metabolite. In addition, several other inactive metabolites are formed. An active metabolite, N-monodesmethyl-rizatriptan, with pharmacological activity similar to that of the parent compound has been identified in small concentrations (14%) in the plasma."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00956"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"299.1521"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic and also in intestinal mucosa."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00956"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"299.1521"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic and also in intestinal mucosa."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00957"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"369.2304"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"GI tract/Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00958"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"371.0445"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00959"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"374.2093"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00960"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"248.1525"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic. In man, 35% to 40% is excreted unchanged in the urine and 60% to 65% is metabolized primarily to hydroxy-metabolites which are excreted as glucuronides and ethereal sulfates."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00960"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"248.1525"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic. In man, 35% to 40% is excreted unchanged in the urine and 60% to 65% is metabolized primarily to hydroxy-metabolites which are excreted as glucuronides and ethereal sulfates."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00961"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"246.1732"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Rapidly metabolized, with only a small percentage of the anesthetic (5 percent to 10 percent) being excreted unchanged in the urine. The liver is the principal site of metabolism, with over 50% of the administered dose being excreted into the bile as metabolites."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00961"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"246.1732"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Rapidly metabolized, with only a small percentage of the anesthetic (5 percent to 10 percent) being excreted unchanged in the urine. The liver is the principal site of metabolism, with over 50% of the administered dose being excreted into the bile as metabolites."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00962"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"305.1277"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Zaleplon is primarily metabolized by aldehyde oxidase."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00962"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"305.1277"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Zaleplon is primarily metabolized by aldehyde oxidase."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00963"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"333.0001"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00964"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"244.0283"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00964"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"244.0283"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00966"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"514.2369"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Minimally metabolized by conjugation to form a pharmacologically inactive acylglucuronide; the glucuronide of the parent compound is the only metabolite that has been identified in human plasma and urine. The cytochrome P450 isoenzymes are not involved in the metabolism of telmisartan."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00966"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"514.2369"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Minimally metabolized by conjugation to form a pharmacologically inactive acylglucuronide; the glucuronide of the parent compound is the only metabolite that has been identified in human plasma and urine. The cytochrome P450 isoenzymes are not involved in the metabolism of telmisartan."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00967"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"310.1237"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00968"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"211.0845"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic, extensively metabolized. The known urinary metabolites are: a-methyldopa mono-0-sulfate; 3-0-methyl-a-methyldopa; 3,4-dihydroxyphenylacetone; a-methyldopamine; 3-0-methyl-a-methyldopamine and their conjugates."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00968"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"211.0845"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic, extensively metabolized. The known urinary metabolites are: a-methyldopa mono-0-sulfate; 3-0-methyl-a-methyldopa; 3,4-dihydroxyphenylacetone; a-methyldopamine; 3-0-methyl-a-methyldopamine and their conjugates."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01144"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"303.9146"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01144"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"303.9146"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01146"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"281.178"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01148"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"391.1784"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01148"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"391.1784"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01149"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"469.2245"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01149"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"469.2245"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01151"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"266.1783"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Desipramine is extensively metabolized in the liver and is cleared mainly by 2-hydroxylation and glucuronidation, whereas 10-hydroxylation is of minor importance. The 2-hydroxylation metabolic pathway of desipramine is under genetic control."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01151"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"266.1783"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Desipramine is extensively metabolized in the liver and is cleared mainly by 2-hydroxylation and glucuronidation, whereas 10-hydroxylation is of minor importance. The 2-hydroxylation metabolic pathway of desipramine is under genetic control."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01154"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"254.1089"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01154"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"254.1089"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01156"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"239.1077"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Reduction of the carbonyl groupand/or hydroxylation of the tert-butyl group of bupropion."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01157"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"369.1801"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic. Preclinical data strongly suggest that the major metabolic pathway is oxidative O-demethylation, followed by conjugation to either glucuronide or the sulfate."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01157"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"369.1801"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic. Preclinical data strongly suggest that the major metabolic pathway is oxidative O-demethylation, followed by conjugation to either glucuronide or the sulfate."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01158"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"242.0544"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"No metabolites have been identified following administration in man and laboratory animals."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01159"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"195.8902"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01159"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"195.8902"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01160"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"475.3145"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Enzymatic dehydrogenation primarily in the maternal lungs and also in the liver."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01161"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"270.1135"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Chloroprocaine is rapidly metabolized in plasma by hydrolysis of the ester linkage by pseudocholinesterase."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01161"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"270.1135"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Chloroprocaine is rapidly metabolized in plasma by hydrolysis of the ester linkage by pseudocholinesterase."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01162"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"387.1907"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic. One of the four metabolites identified (piperazine derivative of terazosin) has antihypertensive activity."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01162"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"387.1907"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic. One of the four metabolites identified (piperazine derivative of terazosin) has antihypertensive activity."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01166"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"369.2165"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic. Cilostazol is extensively metabolized by hepatic cytochrome P-450 enzymes, mainly 3A4, and, to a lesser extent, 2C19, with metabolites largely excreted in urine. Two metabolites are active, with one metabolite appearing to account for at least 50% of the pharmacologic (PDE III inhibition) activity after administration of cilostazol."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00986"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"397.1253"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00987"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"243.0855"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00987"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"243.0855"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00988"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"153.079"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Biotransformation of dopamine proceeds rapidly to yield the principal excretion products, 3-4-dihydroxy-phenylacetic acid (DOPAC) and 3-methoxy-4-hydroxy-phenylacetic acid (homovanillic acid, HVA)."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00988"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"153.079"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Biotransformation of dopamine proceeds rapidly to yield the principal excretion products, 3-4-dihydroxy-phenylacetic acid (DOPAC) and 3-methoxy-4-hydroxy-phenylacetic acid (homovanillic acid, HVA)."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00989"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"250.1681"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Rivastigmine is rapidly metabolized by cholinesterase-mediated hydrolysis."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00989"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"250.1681"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Rivastigmine is rapidly metabolized by cholinesterase-mediated hydrolysis."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00990"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"296.1776"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00990"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"296.1776"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00991"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"293.1052"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic. Ester and ether glucuronide are the major conjugated metabolites of oxaprozin, and do not have significant pharmacologic activity."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00991"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"293.1052"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic. Ester and ether glucuronide are the major conjugated metabolites of oxaprozin, and do not have significant pharmacologic activity."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00993"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"277.0382"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Primarily converted to the active metabolites 6-mercaptopurine and 6-thioinosinic acid."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00993"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"277.0382"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Primarily converted to the active metabolites 6-mercaptopurine and 6-thioinosinic acid."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00995"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"678.1327"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00996"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"171.1259"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"All pharmacological actions following gabapentin administration are due to the activity of the parent compound; gabapentin is not appreciably metabolized in humans."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00819"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"221.9881"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00819"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"221.9881"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00820"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"389.1376"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Tadalafil is predominantly metabolized by CYP3A4 to a catechol metabolite. The catechol metabolite undergoes extensive methylation and glucuronidation to form the methylcatechol and methylcatechol glucuronide conjugate, respectively. In vitro data suggests the metabolites are not expected to be pharmacologically active at observed metabolite concentrations."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00821"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"273.0557"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00822"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"296.0509"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00822"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"296.0509"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00823"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"384.2301"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00823"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"384.2301"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00824"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"194.0804"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00825"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"156.1514"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00825"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"156.1514"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00829"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"284.0716"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic via the Cytochrome P450 enzyme system. The main active metabolite is desmethyldiazepam, in addition to minor active metabolites including temazepam and oxazepam."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00829"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"284.0716"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic via the Cytochrome P450 enzyme system. The main active metabolite is desmethyldiazepam, in addition to minor active metabolites including temazepam and oxazepam."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00830"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"177.1154"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Primarily hepatic (via CYP3A and CYP2D6). Resistant to metabolism by monoamine oxidase. Metabolism involves deamination to para-hydroxyamphetamine and phenylacetone; this latter compound is subsequently oxidize to benzoic acid and excreted as glucuronide or glycine (hippuric acid) conjugate. Smaller amounts of amphetamine are converted to norephedrine by oxidation."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01274"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"344.1736"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Arformoterol was almost entirely metabolized following oral administration of 35 mcg of radiolabeled arformoterol in eight healthy subjects. Direct conjugation of arformoterol with glucuronic acid was the major metabolic pathway. O-Desmethylation is a secondary route catalyzed by the CYP enzymes CYP2D6 and CYP2C19."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01275"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"160.0749"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hydralazine, when administered orally, undergoes extensive first-pass metabolism by genetic polymorphic acetylation, which is responsible for a threefold range of oral bioavailability. Intravenously administered hydralazine does not undergo first-pass metabolism and, therefore, is not affected by acetylator phenotype. After the drug reaches the systemic circulation, it is combined with endogenous aldehydes and ketones, including pyruvic acid, to form hydrazone metabolites. The active metabolites, hydralazine acetonide hydrazone and hydralazine pyruvate hydrazone, are equipotent with the parent, hydralazine."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01275"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"160.0749"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hydralazine, when administered orally, undergoes extensive first-pass metabolism by genetic polymorphic acetylation, which is responsible for a threefold range of oral bioavailability. Intravenously administered hydralazine does not undergo first-pass metabolism and, therefore, is not affected by acetylator phenotype. After the drug reaches the systemic circulation, it is combined with endogenous aldehydes and ketones, including pyruvic acid, to form hydrazone metabolites. The active metabolites, hydralazine acetonide hydrazone and hydralazine pyruvate hydrazone, are equipotent with the parent, hydralazine."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01276"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"4184.0273"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01277"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"7643.5862"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Both the liver and the kidney have been shown to metabolize IGF-1."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01278"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"3946.9207"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Metabolized primarily by the kidneys."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01279"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"55833.1016"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01280"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"297.1073"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"The principal route of metabolism for nelarabine is O-demethylation by adenosine deaminase to form ara-G, which undergoes hydrolysis to form guanine. In addition, some nelarabine is hydrolyzed to form methylguanine, which is O-demethylated to form guanine. Guanine is N-deaminated to form xanthine, which is further oxidized to yield uric acid. Ring opening of uric acid followed by further oxidation results in the formation of allantoin."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01280"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"297.1073"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"The principal route of metabolism for nelarabine is O-demethylation by adenosine deaminase to form ara-G, which undergoes hydrolysis to form guanine. In addition, some nelarabine is hydrolyzed to form methylguanine, which is O-demethylated to form guanine. Guanine is N-deaminated to form xanthine, which is further oxidized to yield uric acid. Ring opening of uric acid followed by further oxidation results in the formation of allantoin."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01281"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"84342.0374"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01282"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"987.4848"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01283"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"293.0619"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic oxidation and hydroxylation via CYP2C9. Three major metabolites have been identified in plasma: 4'-hydroxy-lumiracoxib, 5-carboxy-lumiracoxib, and 4'-hydroxy-5-carboxy-lumiracoxib."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01284"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"2931.5806"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01284"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"2931.5806"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01288"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"303.1471"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic."