 via cytochrome P450 isoenzymes CYP2C9 and CYP3A4. Metabolism is via hydroxylation followed by glucuronidation. The major metabolites have less antidiabetic activity than nateglinide, but the isoprene minor metabolite has antidiabetic activity comparable to that of nateglinide."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00732"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"928.5085"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00733"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"137.0715"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00734"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"410.2118"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB00736"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"367.0967"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01185"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"336.2101"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Presence of 17-alpha alkyl group reduces susceptibility to hepatic enzyme degradation, which slows metabolism and allows oral administration. Inactivation of testosterone occurs primarily in the liver"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01186"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"314.1817"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Extensively hepatic."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01186"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"314.1817"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Extensively hepatic."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01188"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"207.1259"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Glucuronidation is the main metabolic pathway of ciclopirox."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01189"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"168.001"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Minimally biotransformed in the liver in humans (approximately 0.02% of the quantity absorbed)."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01189"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"168.001"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Minimally biotransformed in the liver in humans (approximately 0.02% of the quantity absorbed)."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01191"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"231.1235"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01192"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"301.1314"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Oxymorphone undergoes extensive hepatic metabolism in humans. After a 10 mg oral dose, 49% was excreted over a five-day period in the urine. Of this, 82% was excreted in the first 24 hours after administration. The recovered drug-related products contained the oxymorphone (1.9%), the conjugate of oxymorphone (44.1%), the 6(beta)-carbinol produced by 6-keto reduction of oxymorphone (0.3%), and the conjugates of 6(beta)-carbinol (2.6%) and 6(alpha)-carbinol (0.1%)."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01193"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"336.2049"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Subject to extensive first-pass hepatic biotransformation (primarily to diacetolol)."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01193"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"336.2049"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Subject to extensive first-pass hepatic biotransformation (primarily to diacetolol)."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01194"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"383.0643"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Ophthalmic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01194"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"383.0643"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Ophthalmic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01195"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"414.1378"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic. Flecainide does not undergo any consequential presystemic biotransformation. The two major urinary metabolites are meta-O-dealkylated flecainide (active, but about one-fifth as potent) and the meta-O-dealkylated lactam of flecainide (non-active metabolite)."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01195"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"414.1378"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic. Flecainide does not undergo any consequential presystemic biotransformation. The two major urinary metabolites are meta-O-dealkylated flecainide (active, but about one-fifth as potent) and the meta-O-dealkylated lactam of flecainide (non-active metabolite)."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01246"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"298.1504"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01246"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"298.1504"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01247"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"231.1008"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic and rapid (by oxidation)."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01248"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"807.3466"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic. In vitro drug interaction studies revealed that docetaxel is metabolized by the CYP3A4 isoenzyme (1 major, 3 minor metabolites)."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01248"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"807.3466"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic. In vitro drug interaction studies revealed that docetaxel is metabolized by the CYP3A4 isoenzyme (1 major, 3 minor metabolites)."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01249"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"1549.7133"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Excreted unchanged"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01250"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"302.0539"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Most (98 to 99%) of an oral dose is rapidly converted into two molecules of 5-aminosalicylic acid (5-ASA) by colonic bacteria and the low prevailing redox potential found in this environment. The conversion of olsalazine to mesalamine in the colon is similar to that of sulfasalazine, which is converted into sulfapyridine and mesalamine. Approximately 0.1% of an oral dose of olsalazine is metabolized in the liver to olsalazine-O-sulfate (olsalazine-S)"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01250"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"302.0539"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Most (98 to 99%) of an oral dose is rapidly converted into two molecules of 5-aminosalicylic acid (5-ASA) by colonic bacteria and the low prevailing redox potential found in this environment. The conversion of olsalazine to mesalamine in the colon is similar to that of sulfasalazine, which is converted into sulfapyridine and mesalamine. Approximately 0.1% of an oral dose of olsalazine is metabolized in the liver to olsalazine-O-sulfate (olsalazine-S)"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01251"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"527.209"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01253"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"325.179"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01253"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"325.179"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01222"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"430.2355"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Following absorption, budesonide is subject to high first pass metabolism (80-90%). In vitro experiments in human liver microsomes demonstrate that budesonide is rapidly and extensively biotransformed, mainly by CYP3A4, to its 2 major metabolites, 6b-hydroxy budesonide and 16a- hydroxy prednisolone. The glucocorticoid activity of these metabolites is negligible (\u003c1/100) in relation to that of the parent compound."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01223"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"420.1982"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01224"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"383.1667"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic. The major metabolic pathways are sulfoxidation, mediated by cytochrome P450 3A4 (CYP3A4), and oxidation. The major metabolites of quetiapine are inactive."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01224"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"383.1667"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic. The major metabolic pathways are sulfoxidation, mediated by cytochrome P450 3A4 (CYP3A4), and oxidation. The major metabolites of quetiapine are inactive."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01226"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"1028.561"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Extensive and rapid via enzymatic hydrolysis catalyzed by plasma cholinesterase. Biotransformation may be significantly slowed in patients with abnormal or decreased plasma cholinesterase activity, especially individuals with a homozygous atypical cholinesterase gene abnormality."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01227"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"353.2355"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Levomethadyl acetate undergoes extensive first-pass metabolism to the active demethylated metabolite nor-levomethadyl acetate, which is further demethylated to a second active metabolite, dinor-levomethadyl acetate. These metabolites are more potent than the parent drug."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01227"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"353.2355"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Levomethadyl acetate undergoes extensive first-pass metabolism to the active demethylated metabolite nor-levomethadyl acetate, which is further demethylated to a second active metabolite, dinor-levomethadyl acetate. These metabolites are more potent than the parent drug."}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01228"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"352.2151"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"'null'"}},{"drug":{"type":"uri","value":"http://www4.wiwiss.fu-berlin.de/drugbank/resource/drugs/DB01229"},"mass":{"type":"literal","value":"853.331"},"transform":{"type":"literal","value":"Hepatic. In vitro studies with human liver microsomes and tissue slices showed that paclitaxel was metabolized primarily to 6a-hydrox-yp